\h 1 Shemuel
\toc1 1 Shemuel
\toc2 1 Shemuel
\toc3 1 She
\mt1 1 Shemuel
\is1 Introduction
\ip This document tells about the people of the nation of Yisrael (called ‘Israel’ in older Bibles) at the time when Shemuel (commonly, but wrongly known as ‘Samuel’) was still their teacher through to when Sha'ul (commonly, but wrongly known as ‘Saul’) led them as their first king. It also reveals the reasons why Sha'ul lost his position as king and why God promised to replace him with David.
\ip Long ago before Yisrael had a king, the people regarded their god as their ruler, but eventually they came to the point of asking God to give them a king to lead them. Both the king and the people were subject to God's leading and expected to obey God's laws.
\ip The main theme of this document is showing how peace and prosperity came when the people obeyed God, and if not, it could lead to destruction. And then sometimes God used his people to apply his discipline to other people groups. It pays to note what's written in 2:30 that goes, “I will honour those who honour me, but those who despise me will be side-lined.”
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 Shemuel as Yisrael's judge \ior 1:1–7:17\ior*
\io1 Sha'ul becomes king \ior 8:1–10:27\ior*
\io1 The first years of Sha'ul's reign \ior 11:1–15:35\ior*
\io1 David and Sha'ul \ior 16:1–30:31\ior*
\io1 The death of Sha'ul and his sons \ior 31:1-13\ior*
\rem This is still a very early look into the unfinished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Elkanah and family at Shiloh
\rem /s1 Elkanah and His Family; Samuel's birth and childhood; The Birth of Samuel; Samuel's Birth and Dedication; Elkanah and His Family at Shiloh
\p
\v 1 There was a man¦125380 named Elkanah¦125389 from Ramatayim¦125384-Zofim¦125385 \add who lived\add* in the Efrayimite hill country. (He was an Efrayimite—the son of Yeroham¦125392, son of Elihu¦125395,\x + \xo 1:1 \xt 1 Chr. 6:27,34.\x* son of Tohu¦125398, son of Zuf¦125401.)
\v 2 He had two wives¦125406: Hannah¦125409 (who had no children) and¦125404 Peninnah¦125412 (who had \add several\add* children).
\p
\v 3 \add ≈Every year\add* he would¦125420 \add take his family\add* to Shiloh¦125431 to \add ≈offer sacrifices¦125428\add* to commander¦125430 Yahweh. Eli¦125436 was Yahweh's¦125429 priest¦125439 there, and his two¦125433 sons¦125434 Hofni¦125437 and Pinehas¦125438 \add also served there\add*.
\p
\v 4 on the day¦125443 that Elkanah¦125445 made his sacrifice¦125444, he would give portions¦125453 \add of the cooked meat\add* to Peninnah¦125447 and to all her sons and daughters¦125452,
\v 5 \add ≈but¦125455\add* he'd give a double¦125459 portion¦125457 to Hannah¦125463 because¦125460 he loved¦125464 \add *her\add*, \add even though\add* Yahweh¦125465 had \add ≈not enabled her to have children\add*.
\v 6 Then¦125469 her rival¦125470 would provoke¦125469 her and¦125469 \add ≈try to make her angry\add*, because¦125476 Yahweh¦125479 hadn't \add ≈given her children\add*.
\v 7 And so it \add ≈happened every year\add*. Every time they went to Yahweh's¦125490 \add ≈tent\add*, \add @Peninnah\add* ridiculed him, and \add @Hannah\add* would weep and wouldn't eat.
\v 8 Then¦125497 Elkanah¦125499 would \add ≈ask\add* her, “Hannah¦125501, why are you crying? And why aren't¦125505 you eating¦125506? And why \add ≈are you so upset\add*? Isn't it better \add ≈having me\add* than \add ≈having\add* ten¦125514 sons¦125515?”
\s1 Hannah and Eli
\rem /s1 Hannah and Eli; Hannah's Prayer for a Son
\p
\v 9 \add One time\add* Hannah¦125518 got up after eating and¦125517 drinking at Shiloh¦125521. (Eli¦125524 the priest¦125525 was sitting¦125526 on the seat¦125529 near the entrance of Yahweh's¦125534 \add ≈tent\add*.)
\v 10 \add @Hannah\add* \add ≈was very upset\add*, and she prayed¦125539 to Yahweh¦125542 and wept uncontrollably.
\v 11 Then¦125546 she made a vow, saying, “Commander Yahweh¦125549, if you \add ≈would only notice the misery\add* of your female servant and remember¦125558 me. Don't¦125559 \add ≈ignore\add* \add *me\add*. \add ≈Help\add* your female servant \add ≈to give birth to a son\add*, then¦125546 I'll¦125569 give him to \add *you\add* \add ≈for his entire¦125571\add* life¦125574, and \add ≈he'll never have his hair cut\add*.”\x + \xo 1:11: \xt Num 6:5.\x*
\p
\v 12 As she¦125585 continued praying to Yahweh¦125588, Eli¦125589 was watching¦125590 her mouth¦125593.
\v 13 \add ≈Now\add* Hannah was \add ≈praying from her heart¦125600\add*, and¦125595 her lips¦125602 were moving \add ≈but¦125595 she wasn't speaking¦125597 aloud\add*. Eli¦125608 thought \add she must be\add* drunk¦125609,
\v 14 and¦125611 \add ≈scolded\add* her, “You shouldn't be getting drunk¦125617. Get rid of your¦125621 wine.”
\p
\v 15 “No, my master¦125628,” Hannah¦125625 replied¦125624, “I'm a woman¦125629 \add ≈who's suffering deep down\add*. I haven't drunk¦125637 any wine or intoxicating drink¦125635, \add ≈but¦125624 rather\add* I've¦125637 been \add ≈sharing my inner issues\add* with Yahweh¦125628.
\v 16 Don't¦125645 think that your¦125650 female servant¦125650 is \add just\add* a worthless¦125654 woman, because¦125655 just now I¦125660 was \add ≈praying as a result of my terrible anguish¦125658 and¦125659 frustration\add*.”
\p
\v 17 “\add Then¦125665\add* go¦125668 in peace¦125669,” Eli¦125666 replied. “And may Yisrael's¦125671 god¦125670 grant¦125672 your¦125675 request that¦125676 you¦125677 \add ≈presented to\add* him¦125678.”
\p
\v 18 “\add ≈Please assume the best of\add* your female servant¦125682,” said¦125680 \add @Hannah\add*, then¦125680 she¦125681 went¦125685 on¦125684 her way¦125687 and \add ≈had something to eat\add*, and \add ≈something had changed in¦125684 her demeanor\add*.
\s1 Shemu'el's birth and dedication
\rem /s1 Samuel's Birth and Dedication
\p
\v 19 They \add all\add* gut up early¦125697 the \add next\add* morning¦125698 and worshipped¦125699 Yahweh \add again\add*, then¦125697 they¦125703 returned¦125702 to their¦125706 home at¦125698 Ramah¦125707. Then¦125697 Elkanah¦125709 \add ≈slept with¦125698\add* his wife Hannah¦125712, and Yahweh \add ≈answered her¦125714 prayer\add*.
\v 20 \add @Hannah\add* got pregnant¦125720, and¦125717 in due course, she gave¦125722 birth¦125722 to a son. She named him ‘Shemu'el’ \add (meaning ‘heard by God’, usually changed to ‘Samuel¦125728’ in English)\add* because¦125729 she had requested him from Yahweh¦125730.
\rem /s1 Hannah Dedicates Samuel
\p
\v 21 \add At the usual time¦125744,\add* Elkanah¦125735 and all his household¦125738 went \add to Shiloh\add* to \add ≈offer¦125739 their sacrifices¦125743 and fulfill their vows\add* to Yahweh¦125740.
\v 22 \add ≈But¦125749\add* Hannah \add ≈didn't¦125750 accompany them\add* because¦125752 she'd told her husband, “I'll¦125759 \add come and\add* bring¦125759 him when he's weaned¦125757. Then¦125749 he can be \add ≈taken in to\add* Yahweh¦125764 and \add ≈permanently\add* remain¦125765 there¦125766.”
\v 23 “Do \add ≈what you think's best\add*,” her husband¦125774 \add ≈replied\add*. “Stay until you've weaned him. May Yahweh¦125785 \add ≈do¦125775 whatever he said¦125771\add* \add to you\add*.” \add ≈So¦125771\add* \add *she\add* stayed \add behind\add* and¦125771 breastfed her son until she \add ≈eventually\add* weaned him.
\p
\v 24 \add The next year,\add* after she'd weaned¦125803 him, she took¦125800 the young¦125804 boy with \add #them\add*, along with three¦125805 bulls¦125804, a \add ≈sack\add* of flour¦125808, and¦125800 a \add ≈container\add* of wine. Then¦125800 she brought¦125811 him to Yahweh's¦125814 \add ≈tent\add* in¦125804 Shiloh¦125815,
\v 25 and¦125823 after slaughtering¦125819 the bull¦125822, they took the boy¦125826 in to Eli¦125829.
\v 26 “Please, my master,” \add @Hannah\add* said. “As your spirit lives, my master, I'm the woman¦125838 who was standing with¦125841 you here to pray to Yahweh¦125833.
\v 27 I prayed¦125851 \add ≈that¦125858 Yahweh¦125853 would give me a son, and he did, and this is him\add*.
\v 28 Also¦125862, I dedicated him¦125864 to Yahweh all his life, so he's now given to Yahweh.”
\p Then¦125862 \add #they\add* worshipped¦125874 Yahweh there¦125875.
\c 2
\s1 Hannah's praise
\rem /s1 Hannah's Prayer; Hannah's Prayer of Praise
\q1
\v 1 Then Hannah¦125880 prayed¦125879, saying,\x + \xo 2:1-10: \xt Luk 1:46-55.\x*
\q1 “\add ≈I'm so happy with¦125884\add* Yahweh.
\q1 My strength comes from Yahweh.
\q1 I can \add ≈loudly refute\add* my enemies¦125892.
\q1 Because I'm \add ≈so pleased that you rescued me\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 2 No one \add else\add* is holy like Yahweh¦125900.
\q1 ≈There's¦125897 certainly no one else who's like you¦125903.
\q1 No one else \add is like a\add* rock¦125905 \add to me\add* like our¦125906 god¦125906 is.
\q1
\v 3 You all shouldn't¦125908 speak¦125911 so proudly.
\q1 ≈\add ≡Don't\add* let arrogance¦125915 come out of your¦125916 mouths¦125916.
\q1 Because Yahweh¦125920 is a god¦125918 of knowledge¦125919,
\q2 and¦125921 all actions are weighed by him.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Warriors have their bows shattered¦125928,
\q1 but¦125929 those¦125929 who stumbled¦125929 \add ≈are given extra\add* strength¦125931.
\q1
\v 5 Those who had been satisfied \add now\add* hire themselves¦125935 out for food,
\q2 but the hungry¦125936 people \add ≈no longer lack\add*.
\q1 The woman who'd been childless¦125942, gives birth to seven¦125942,
\q2 but the woman with¦125934 many sons¦125944 \add ≈is depressed\add*.
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh¦125947 puts to death and he¦125950 makes¦125949 alive¦125949.
\q1 ≈He¦125951 sends down to the grave and brings¦125950 up.
\q1
\v 7 Yahweh¦125954 makes poor and makes rich.
\q1 ≈He humbles, and he also honours.
\q1
\v 8 He lifts the poor up from the dust,
\q1 ≈and lifts up the needy¦125967 from the ash heap
\q1 so they can sit¦125968 with influential \add people\add*
\q1 ≈and be given the seat¦125972 of honour¦125973.
\q1 The earth's¦125978 foundations belong to Yahweh¦125976—
\q1 he was the one who placed the world¦125981 on top of them.
\q1
\v 9 He \add ≈watches over\add* those who are loyal to him,
\q1 but¦125987 wicked¦125987 \add people\add* will die in¦125988 the darkness¦125988,
\q2 because¦125990 \add ≈we\add* don't¦125992 succeed through our own strength¦125993.
\q1
\v 10 It's Yahweh who shatters¦125999 our enemies.
\q1 He thunders¦126005 against¦126002 \add #them\add* from the heavens¦126004.
\q1 Yahweh will judge¦126007 the \add ≈entire\add* earth¦126010,
\q1 and will give strength¦126013 to the king¦126014,
\q1 and will \add ≈give victory\add* to his chosen one.”
\rem /s1 Eli's Wicked Sons
\p
\v 11 Then Elkanah¦126021 \add and his family\add* returned to \add #their\add* home¦126025 in Ramah¦126022, but¦126020 young Shemuel \add stayed to\add* help Eli¦126035 the priest¦126036 serve Yahweh¦126031.
\s1 Eli's greedy sons
\rem /s1 The Sons of Eli; Eli's Wicked Sons
\p
\v 12 \add ≈Now\add* Eli's¦126039 sons were \add ≈worthless¦126041 scoundrels\add* who didn't¦126042 obey Yahweh¦126046.
\v 13 It was the custom¦126048 of those priests¦126049 that when anyone was offering a sacrifice¦126056 and¦126048 as soon as the meat was boiling¦126061, the priest's servant¦126059 would come¦126058 along holding a three-pronged fork
\v 14 and¦126069 stick it into the pot or pan. The priest¦126082 would then¦126069 \add ≈claim\add* whatever the fork brought¦126079 up. That's what they did¦126085 to all the Israelis who came to Shiloh¦126091 \add to offer sacrifices\add*.
\v 15 Also, before¦126094 the fat \add was removed to\add* burn, the priest's servant¦126101 would say to the person¦126104 sacrificing¦126105, “Give some meat to the priest—he doesn't want just boiled¦126115 meat \add all the time\add*—he'll take some raw meat \add as well\add* to roast¦126108.”
\p
\v 16 But¦126121 if the person tried to say, “Once the fat has all been burnt, then take whatever you want for yourself¦126130,” then he'd \add ≈assert\add*, “No, you'd better give¦126140 it \add right\add* now or I'll take it by force¦126145.”
\v 17 And \add so\add* the actions of those young¦126149 men were very wicked in Yahweh's¦126155 mind because¦126156 they \add just\add* treated¦126157 sacrifices to Yahweh with contempt¦126157.
\s1 Young Shemuel starts serving at Shiloh
\rem /s1 The Child Samuel at Shiloh; Samuel at Shiloh
\p
\v 18 \add ≈By now\add* Shemuel was serving¦126164 Yahweh¦126168, dressed in a priestly uniform made from linen¦126172.
\v 19 \add ≈Each¦126190 year\add* his mother¦126179 would make a small¦126175 robe¦126174 for him and¦126174 bring it when she came with¦126185 her husband¦126188 to offer¦126189 \add #their sacrifices¦126192\add*.
\v 20 Eli¦126196 would bless¦126195 Elkanah¦126199 and his wife and say, “May Yahweh \add ≈give more children for you through\add* this woman¦126210 in place¦126212 of the \add boy\add* \add ≈dedicated\add* to Yahweh.” Then¦126195 they'd return home¦126218.
\p
\v 21 \add ≈So\add* Yahweh visited¦126222 Hannah¦126226 and she went on to give birth¦126228 to three¦126229 \add other\add* sons¦126231 and two¦126232 daughters¦126233. \add ≈Meanwhile,\add* \add ≈young\add* Shemuel¦126236 \add ≈grew up serving\add* Yahweh.
\s1 Eli and his children
\rem /s1 Eli and His Sons; Prophecy against Eli's Household
\p
\v 22 \add ≈Now\add* Eli¦126242 was very old and he had heard¦126245 all about what his sons¦126251 were doing¦126250 to the Israeli people, and \add also\add* that they were sleeping around with the women¦126261 who worked at the sacred¦126264 tent¦126264 entrance.
\v 23 “Why are you \add two\add* doing¦126270 all these things that I keep hearing¦126275 about—these evil things \add ≈affecting\add* all those people¦126283?” he \add ≈asked\add* them.
\v 24 “No, my sons¦126287. The reports¦126292 that¦126293 I keep hearing¦126295 Yahweh's¦126299 people¦126297 passing on aren't¦126289 good¦126291!
\v 25 If someone sins against a person, then God¦126307 will mediate¦126306 for him, but¦126306 if someone sins against Yahweh, who will \add ≈speak up\add* for that person?” \add ≈But¦126306\add* they wouldn't listen¦126318 to their¦126320 father's¦126320 advice because¦126321 Yahweh wanted to put them to death¦126325.
\p
\v 26 \add ≈Meanwhile\add* the boy¦126327 Shemuel was growing¦126330 \add up\add*, and his good¦126331 \add behaviour pleased\add* both Yahweh¦126335 and \add ≈the people¦126339\add*.\x + \xo 2:26: \xt Luk 2:52.\x*
\s1 The prophecy against Eli's family
\rem /s1 The Prophecy against Eli's Family; A Warning for Eli's Family; Prophecy Against the House of Eli
\p
\v 27 \add ≈One day\add* one of God's \add prophets\add* came¦126342 to Eli¦126348 and told him, “This is what Yahweh¦126353 says¦126352, ‘Did\add n't\add* I clearly reveal myself¦126355 to your ancestors when they were Far'oh's \add ≈slaves\add* in¦126361 Egypt¦126361?
\v 28 And out of all the Israeli¦126369 tribes¦126369, I chose¦126365 \add @your ancestor\add* to be a priest¦126372 to me, to go¦126373 up on my¦126381 altar¦126376, to burn¦126377 incense¦126378, \add and¦126382\add* to \add ≈wear the sacred apron in front of me\add*. Plus I gave¦126382 your ancestors \add the rights to\add* all the burnt offerings¦126389 made by the \add ≈Israelis¦126370\add*.\x + \xo 2:28: a \xt Exo 28:1-4; \xo b \xt Lev 7:35-36.\x*
\v 29 Why do you \add ≈disrespect\add* sacrifices¦126395 and offerings that¦126397 I commanded¦126398 \add the people\add* to bring to me \add ≈at¦126395 my residence\add*. And \add ≡why do\add* you honour¦126400 your sons more than me by fattening yourselves¦126405 from the best of every¦126407 offering of my people Yisrael¦126410?’
\p
\v 30 “Therefore, this is what Yahweh¦126416 the god¦126417 of Yisrael¦126418 declares: ‘I indeed¦126419 said \add ≈your ancestors and then your descendants\add* would \add ≈serve me forever¦126428\add*.’ But¦126422 now \add ≈I declare this\add* \add instead\add*: ‘\add ≈This cannot continue\add* because¦126435 I will honour those who honour me, but¦126422 those who despise¦126439 me will be \add ≈side-lined\add*.
\v 31 Listen¦126445, the time¦126443 is coming¦126444 when I'll¦126453 \add ≈bring you and your relatives to an end—none of your men will die of old age\add*
\v 32 and¦126468 you'll see¦126458 distress¦126459 in my residence. I'll¦126465 do¦126464 good¦126464 for \add the rest of\add* Yisrael¦126467, but¦126458 there'll never be another old man¦126471 in your¦126472 home.
\v 33 I won't¦126478 \add ≈finish\add* them off near my altar¦126483. Your eyes¦126487 will fail¦126484 and¦126477 you'll grieve¦126488 \add ≈inside\add*, but¦126477 your \add adult sons\add* will die¦126496.
\v 34 This will be the sign¦126502: it'll \add ≈turn out\add* that¦126503 your¦126508 two¦126507 sons¦126508 Hofni¦126511 and¦126512 Finehas will both¦126507 die¦126515 on the same day¦126513.\x + \xo 2:34: \xt 1Sam 4:11.\x*
\v 35 Then¦126518 I'll \add ≈select\add* another man to be a faithful priest¦126520 to me—he'll \add ≈follow my desires and do what's in¦126523 my mind¦126523\add*. I'll make \add ≈him and his faithful descendants to continue and to always serve me\add*.
\v 36 Any of your¦126543 descendants who remain¦126542 \add ≈alive\add* will have to go to him to request food¦126551 and money and \add ≈beg\add* for some priestly¦126558 work to earn \add ≈something to eat¦126559\add*.’ ”
\c 3
\s1 Yahweh calls Shemuel
\rem /s1 The Lord Appears to Samuel; Samuel's Calling and Prophetic Activity; The Lord Speaks to Samuel; The Lord Calls Samuel
\p
\v 1 \add ≈Meanwhile young\add* Shemuel was serving¦126567 Yahweh \add ≈under\add* Eli¦126572. In those¦126579 days¦126578, Yahweh rarely \add ≈spoke to the people or sent visions¦126581\add*.
\p
\v 2 \add ≈By that time,\add* Eli's¦126588 \add ≈sight was very poor\add*—he could barely see¦126598. \add One night when\add* he was sleeping in his place,\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Some translations use ‘room’ here instead of ‘place’, but it could easily be that he had a small bed in a corner somewhere.\f*
\v 3 and¦126604 Shemuel¦126604 was lying¦126605 down in Yahweh's¦126607 tent (where¦126608 the box¦126611 was that contained the \add ≈stone slabs\add*), \add ≈but¦126600\add* God's lamp was still burning.
\v 4 Yahweh¦126616 called out to Shemuel¦126619, and he \add ≈called back\add*, “I'm here. \add Coming.\add*”
\v 5 Then¦126623 he ran¦126623 to Eli¦126626 and said, “Here I am, because¦126629 you called me.”
\p “I didn't¦126634 call,” he said. “Go back \add and\add* lie down.” \add ≈So¦126623\add* he went¦126639 \add back\add* and laid down.
\p
\v 6 Yahweh¦126644 called again¦126646, “Shemuel¦126647.”
\p Shemuel got up and went¦126650 to Eli¦126653 and said, “Here I am, because¦126656 you called me.”
\p “I didn't¦126660 call¦126645, my son,” he said, “Go back \add and\add* lie down.”
\p
\v 7 (\add At this point\add*, Shemuel didn't¦126668 \add really\add* know Yahweh, because Yahweh hadn't revealed¦126674 \add himself\add* to him before.)
\v 8 Then Yahweh called Shemuel a third¦126685 time¦126685. He got up and went¦126687 to Eli¦126690 and said¦126691, “Here I am, because¦126693 you called me.”
\p Then Eli realised that it was Yahweh who was calling the boy¦126701
\v 9 and he told Shemuel, “Go \add and\add* lie down. Then¦126703 if he calls to you \add again\add*, \add ≈just answer\add*, ‘Speak, Yahweh¦126715, because¦126716 your servant¦126718 is listening¦126717.’ ” \add ≈So¦126703\add* Shemuel went¦126719 and laid down \add again\add* in¦126722 his place¦126722.
\p
\v 10 Then¦126724 Yahweh¦126725 came¦126724 and \add ≈stood¦126726 nearby\add* and called¦126727 \add ≈like he'd done the other\add* times¦126730, “Shemuel, Shemuel.”
\p “Speak,” Shemuel¦126731 said¦126734, “because¦126737 your¦126739 servant¦126739 is listening¦126738.”
\p
\v 11 “Listen,” Yahweh¦126743 told \add *him\add*, “I'm about¦126749 to do something in¦126751 Yisrael¦126751 that¦126752 \add ≈will make everyone's¦126753 ears tingle¦126756 when they hear it\add*.
\v 12 \add ≈When it happens\add*, I'll¦126762 \add ≈do everything that I've¦126770 said about Eli¦126765 and¦126775 his family\add*, from the beginning¦126774 to the end¦126775.
\v 13 I've told him \add already\add* that I'm about to \add ≈eternally\add* judge¦126781 his \add ≈household¦126785\add* because \add ≈he knew¦126792 about the evil that was being done\add*, because his sons¦126797 were \add ≈bringing¦126795 curses¦126795 on¦126789\add* themselves and¦126777 he didn't¦126798 scold them.
\v 14 That's why I've promised Eli's¦126805 \add ≈family\add* that \add *their\add* disobedience can \add ≈never\add* be forgiven by means of sacrifices¦126813 or offerings¦126814.
\s1 Shemuel tells Eli what Yahweh said
\rem /s1 Samuel Speaks for the Lord
\p
\v 15 Then¦126819 Shemuel¦126831 lay down \add again\add* until¦126821 the morning¦126823 when he opened¦126824 the doors¦126827 of Yahweh's¦126830 house¦126828 \add as usual\add*, \add ≈but¦126819\add* \add *he\add* was scared to tell Eli¦126839 about the vision¦126836.
\v 16 \add ≈But¦126841\add* Eli¦126842 called¦126841 \add *him\add*, “Shemuel¦126845, my son.”
\p “Here I am,” he \add ≈answered\add*.
\p
\v 17 “What did he tell you?” \add @Eli\add* \add ≈asked\add*. “Don't¦126858 hide it from me. May God¦126867 \add ≈punish you severely\add* if you hide a \add single\add* word¦126874 from \add ≈everything\add* he told you.”
\v 18 \add ≈So¦126883\add* Shemuel¦126886 told him \add ≈everything\add* without hiding anything. Then¦126883 Eli \add ≈answered\add*, “It's Yahweh¦126896. May he do¦126901 \add ≈whatever he thinks best\add*.”
\p
\v 19 \add ≈As\add* Shemuel¦126905 grew up, Yahweh¦126906 was with him and nothing he said failed to happen,
\v 20 \add ≈so\add* all Yisrael¦126920 from Dan¦126921 \add in the north\add* and¦126922 as far as Beer¦126924-Sheva¦126925 \add in the south\add* knew¦126917 that Shemuel¦126928 was a faithful prophet¦126929 of Yahweh¦126930.
\v 21 \add Over time,\add* Yahweh¦126933 continued¦126932 to appear¦126934 in¦126943 Shiloh¦126935 and¦126932 to reveal¦126938 \add ≈his plans through\add* Shemuel¦126942
\c 4
\rem /s1 The Capture of the Covenant Box
\nb
\v 1 and Shemuel's¦126951 messages¦126949 went¦126955 to all Yisrael¦126954.
\s1 The capture of the box with the agreement
\rem /s1 The Philistines Capture the Ark; The struggle with the Philistines; The Ark of God Captured
\p \add At that time,\add* Yisrael went to war against the Philistines. They camped at Eben-Ezer, and the Philistines camped at Afek¦126967.
\v 2 Then¦126969 the Philistines lined up to meet Yisrael and the battle spread¦126973. Yisrael was defeated¦126975 by the Philistines and they killed¦126979 about¦126982 4,000 warriors in the battle line in the countryside¦126981.
\v 3 \add ≈As\add* the people\f + \fr 4:3 \ft It seems that most translations assume that this is referring to the remainder of the warriors staggering back into the camp (which is certainly a possibility), but we've interpreted it as the people coming together (with their elders as it says), to try to figure out why they were defeated. (Similarly for v4.)\f* came¦126986 into the camp, the \add ≈Israeli¦126992\add* elders \add ≈asked\add* \add themselves\add*, “Why did Yahweh \add ≈allow the Philistines¦126999 to slaughter\add* us today? Let's get the box¦127005 from Shiloh¦127002 that contains \add the stone slabs with\add* Yahweh's¦126996 agreement¦127006 \add on¦127009 them\add*. \add ≈Once it's amongst us\add*, \add surely\add* it'll save¦127010 us from \add ≈defeat¦126995 by\add* our¦127012 enemies¦127012.”
\v 4 \add ≈So¦127014\add* the people¦127015 sent¦127014 \add <men\add* to Shiloh¦127016 and from there they¦127017 lifted the box containing Yahweh's¦127023 agreement \add and the sacred platform\add* with the winged creatures.\f + \fr 4:4 \ft And with Commander Yahweh sitting on, above, or between them.\f* Eli's¦127031 two¦127028 sons¦127029, Hofni¦127037 and Finehas, were there with the box containing God's agreement.\x + \xo 4:4: \xt Exo 25:22.\x*
\p
\v 5 \add ≈As¦127052\add* the box¦127042 with Yahweh's¦127045 agreement¦127043 \add ≈was carried\add* into the camp¦127048, all the \add ≈Israelis¦127050\add* shouted¦127053 so loud that the ground shook¦127055.
\v 6 When¦127058 the Philistines¦127059 heard¦127058 the \add ≈noise\add*, they said¦127064, “What's that loud shout in¦127070 the Hebrews' camp?” Then¦127058 they realised that the box¦127074 \add ≈with¦127070 Yahweh's¦127075 covenant\add* had come¦127076 into their camp
\v 7 and¦127081 they were afraid saying, “A god¦127086 has come¦127085 into the \add Israeli\add* camp¦127089.” Then¦127081 they said, “\add ≈This means trouble\add*, because¦127083 it wasn't like this yesterday¦127097 \add ≈or the previous days¦127098\add*.
\v 8 \add ≈Oh dear\add*! Who will rescue¦127103 us from these mighty¦127106 gods? Those are the gods who struck¦127111 the Egyptians with¦127118 every plague¦127117 in¦127118 the wilderness¦127118.
\v 9 Now we Philistines¦127123 \add ≈really need to fight with passion\add*, because if we don't, we'll ending up serving the Hebrews¦127126 just like they've had to serve¦127125 us. \add ≈Now, gather your courage and fight for your lives\add*.”
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So¦127134\add* the Philistines¦127135 fought¦127134 \add hard\add*, and Yisrael was \add ≈defeated¦127136\add*. \add ≈Many warriors fled¦127138 back to their tents¦127140\add* and it¦127141 was a \add ≈terrible\add* slaughter¦127142—30,000 foot¦127149 soldiers¦127149 from Yisrael were killed.
\v 11 \add ≈As well as that,\add* the box¦127151 with God's \add agreement\add* was taken¦127153, and Eli's¦127157 two¦127154 sons¦127155 Hofni¦127159 and Finehas were killed.
\s1 The death of Eli
\rem /s1 Death of Eli; The Death of Eli
\p
\v 12 A Benyamite man¦127163 ran¦127162 from the battle¦127166 line¦127166 back to Shiloh¦127168 that day¦127169. His clothes¦127171 were torn and¦127162 \add ≈he was covered in dirt\add*.
\v 13 \add ≈When¦127178\add* he arrived¦127178 \add there\add*, Eli¦127180 was sitting¦127181 on¦127200 a roadside seat watching¦127188, because¦127189 he was \add ≈very concerned\add* about the \add ≈sacred chest¦127195\add*. As¦127196 the man entered the city with¦127200 the news, all the \add ≈people\add* started \add ≈wailing loudly\add*.
\v 14 \add ≈As\add* Eli heard the wailing, he \add ≈asked\add*, “\add ≈What's that big commotion¦127215\add*?” \add ≈So¦127206\add* the man¦127217 ran over to Eli to \add ≈tell him the news\add*.
\v 15 \add ≈At that time\add*, Eli was ninety-eight years old, and¦127223 he \add ≈stared straight ahead\add* because he couldn't see¦127233.
\v 16 The man¦127236 told \add *him\add*, “I'm the one who's \add just\add* come¦127241 from the battle line. I \add had to\add* fled from the battle line today.”
\p “What was the issue, my son?” \add @Eli\add* \add ≈asked\add*.
\p
\v 17 “Yisrael¦127262 fled¦127261 \add ≈defeated\add* from the Philistines¦127264,” said¦127260 the messenger¦127259. “And also, there has been a terrible slaughter¦127266 of our people¦127269. \add ≈What's more\add*, your¦127273 two¦127272 sons¦127273, Hofni¦127275 and Finehas, \add ≈were killed\add*, and the \add ≈sacred chest¦127277 has been captured\add*.”
\p
\v 18 \add ≈The moment\add* he mentioned¦127283 the capturing of God's box¦127287, \add @Eli\add* fell¦127289 backwards¦127293 off the seat¦127292 beside the city gate¦127297. Because he was old and \add very\add* heavy¦127305, his neck broke¦127298 and he died¦127300. Eli had \add ≈led\add* Yisrael¦127310 for forty¦127311 years.
\s1 The death of Finehas' widow
\rem /s1 The Death of the Widow of Phinehas
\p
\v 19 \add ≈Now\add* Finehas' wife (\add @Eli's\add* daughter-in-law) was pregnant¦127318—about to give birth—\add ≈when\add* she heard¦127320 the news¦127323 about the \add ≈sacred chest¦127327\add* being¦127326 taken¦127326, and that \add both\add* her father-in-law and her husband¦127331 had died. \add ≈At that moment,\add* she knelt down and gave birth \add ≈because¦127334 the birth pains¦127338 suddenly started\add*.
\v 20 \add ≈But she was dying\add*, and¦127340 the women who stood over her said¦127342, “Don't¦127345 be afraid¦127347, because¦127348 you've¦127350 given birth to a son.” \add ≈But\add* she didn't¦127345 answer¦127352 \add ≈or pay any attention¦127343\add*.
\v 21 She named the boy¦127359 ‘Ikabod’ \add (which means ‘no glory’)\add*, saying, “The glory has departed from Yisrael¦127366,” about the \add ≈sacred chest¦127370\add* being¦127369 taken¦127369, and about \add the deaths of\add* her father-in-law and her husband¦127375.
\v 22 Then¦127377 she said¦127377, “The¦127384 glory¦127379 has departed from Yisrael¦127380, because¦127381 the \add ≈sacred chest¦127383\add* has been taken¦127382.”
\c 5
\s1 The sacred chest gets carried to Ashdod
\rem /s1 The Philistines and the Ark; The Ark in Philistia; The Ark in Ashdod and Ekron; The Covenant Box among the Philistines
\p
\v 1 Now the Philistines¦127387 had taken the \add ≈sacred chest¦127390\add*, and they¦127388 took¦127388 it¦127392 from Eben-Ezer to Ashdod¦127395
\v 2 \add ≈where\add* they carried \add *it\add* into the temple of \add their god\add* Dagon and¦127397 placed¦127407 it beside¦127409 \add a statue of\add* Dagon.
\v 3 Early the next day¦127414 \add ≈when\add* the Ashdodites got up, \add ≈to their horror\add* Dagon¦127416 had fallen to the ground on its face \add ≈in front¦127420 of\add* Yahweh's¦127422 box¦127421. \add ≈So¦127412\add* they \add ≈stood it up\add* and¦127412 returned¦127427 it to its place¦127429.
\v 4 \add But¦127431\add* then¦127431 the next morning¦127432, \add ≈more shock\add*: Dagon¦127435 had fallen to the ground on its face \add ≈in¦127432 front¦127439 of\add* Yahweh's¦127441 box¦127440 \add ≈but¦127431 now\add* \add *its\add* hands¦127447 and head were cut off, \add and were lying¦127436\add* on the threshhold¦127451. Only \add ≈its body remained¦127454 intact\add*.
\v 5 (\add ≈Because of that\add*, until¦127476 this day¦127477 Dagon's priests¦127463 and¦127465 everyone who enters Dagon's temple in¦127475 Ashdod¦127475 won't¦127460 tread¦127462 on¦127475 the threshhold¦127473.)
\s1 God punishes the Philistine cities
\p
\v 6 Yahweh¦127484 \add ≈caused a lot of trouble\add* for the \add people of\add* Ashdod¦127487 and he terrified¦127488 them. He¦127496 \add ≈caused\add* them and \add ≈the people in¦127491 the region\add* to \add ≈get lumpy growths on¦127491 their skin\add*.
\v 7 \add ≈When¦127501\add* the \add ≈leaders\add* in Ashdod¦127504 \add ≈saw what was happening\add*, they said, “The god¦127513 of Yisrael's¦127514 box¦127512 mustn't stay with us because¦127505 \add ≈he's punishing\add* \add both\add* us and¦127501 our god Dagon¦127522.”
\v 8 \add ≈So\add* they sent¦127525 for all the Philistine rulers¦127531, and¦127525 \add when they'd assembled\add* they \add ≈asked\add*, “What should¦127543 we¦127537 do with the god of Yisrael's¦127540 box?”
\p “Let the god of Yisrael's box be taken around to Gat¦127542,” they \add ≈decided\add*. \add ≈So\add* they moved¦127547 \add *it\add* \add there\add*.
\v 9 After \add ≈it arrived\add*, Yahweh¦127563 \add ≈started punishing Gat\add* and \add both\add* young¦127574 and old men in¦127565 the city started getting growths on¦127565 their skin, and it caused a huge¦127567 panic¦127566.
\v 10 \add ≈So¦127583\add* they¦127597 sent¦127583 God's box to Ekron¦127588, \add ≈but¦127583\add* as soon as \add *it\add* entered \add *the city\add*, the Ekronites cried¦127594 out, “\add Hey!\add* They've brought¦127597 the god¦127602 of Yisrael's¦127603 box here to \add ≈kill¦127604 us\add* and¦127583 our¦127607 people¦127607.”
\v 11 \add ≈So¦127609\add* they sent¦127609 for the Philistine rulers¦127615, and \add ≈when they'd assembled\add* they said¦127617, “Send the god¦127622 of Yisrael's¦127623 box¦127621 away. \add ≈Send it back\add* to its place¦127625 \add ≈so\add* it won't¦127626 \add ≈cause us and our people¦127632 to die\add*.” Because there was a \add ≈deadly panic¦127636 all over\add* the city¦127641 \add as\add* \add ≈God's punishment there was severe\add*
\v 12 and¦127657 the men¦127648 who didn't¦127650 die suffered with¦127654 the growths. \add ≈So¦127648\add* the \add ≈people's\add* cry¦127658 went¦127657 up to the heavens¦127660.
\c 6
\s1 Sending the chest back to Yisrael
\rem /s1 The Return of the Covenant Box; The Ark Returned to Israel; The Philistines Return the Ark
\p
\v 1 \add ≈So¦127662 it was that\add* Yahweh's¦127665 box¦127663 was \add held\add* in¦127666 Philistine¦127667 territory¦127666 for seven¦127668 months¦127669,
\v 2 then¦127671 the Philistines¦127672 called¦127671 \add &their\add* priests¦127673 and diviners¦127674 and \add ≈asked\add*, “What should we do¦127678 with¦127682 Yahweh's¦127680 box¦127679? \add ≈Show us how we can get it¦127683 back\add* away to its¦127684 place¦127684.”
\p
\v 3 “If you're going to send the god¦127693 of Yisrael's¦127694 box¦127692 back,” they \add ≈responded¦127686\add*, “don't¦127695 send it \add ≈back by itself\add*, but¦127686 definitely return it to him with a guilt¦127705 offering¦127705. Then¦127686 you'll all be healed¦127707, and¦127686 you'll all \add ≈understand why you've been suffering\add*.”
\p
\v 4 “What guilt¦127719 offering¦127719 should we \add ≈send with it\add*?” they \add ≈asked\add*.
\p “Five gold \add replicas of the\add* tumours, and five gold mice,” they said. “That's the number¦127724 of \add *our\add* rulers, because¦127734 \add ≈the same\add* plague¦127736 \add ≈affected both them and the people\add*.
\v 5 Make replicas of the tumours and replicas of the mice that have been destroying the land, and it will honour¦127754 Yisrael's¦127753 god¦127752. Then¦127741 perhaps¦127755 he will \add ≈less his punishment that's been\add* on you and on your gods¦127762 and on your land.
\v 6 Why \add ≈have you¦127767 all been so stubborn\add* just like the Egyptians when Pharaoh¦127774 made \add ≈them stubborn\add*? When¦127766 \add @Yisrael's god\add* dealt¦127780 severely with them, didn't¦127778 \add @the Egyptians\add* send¦127782 \add @the Israelis\add* away and¦127766 they¦127772 went¦127783?
\v 7 \add ≈So¦127785\add* now, \add ≈get a brand\add* new cart with¦127804 two nursing¦127793 cows \add ≈which¦127794 have never had a yoke on¦127804 their necks\add*. Hitch¦127801 the cows to the cart \add ≈but¦127785\add* take their calves¦127806 \add ≈back to their pen\add*.
\v 8 Then¦127810 pick up Yahweh's¦127814 box and put it into the cart \add ≈along with\add* the gold¦127823 objects that¦127824 you are \add ≈sending with it\add* as a guilt¦127827 offering¦127827 \add ≈put in¦127829 another container beside the box\add*. \add ≈Finally\add*, send¦127831 \add @the cart\add* away and \add off\add* it will go¦127833.
\v 9 Then¦127835 watch¦127835 \add <it\add*. If it \add ≈heads towards\add* Beyt-Shemesh¦127842 \add ≈in¦127859 Israeli territory, then it's their god that caused this terrible calamity\add*. \add ≈But¦127835\add* if not, then we'll know¦127854 that \add ≈it wasn't the Israeli god, and¦127835 that\add* it \add all\add* happened¦127862 to us by chance¦127860.”
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So¦127874 they followed those instructions\add*: they took¦127868 two¦127869 cows¦127870 \add ≈that had been feeding calves\add* and¦127865 hitched¦127872 them to the \add new\add* cart, but¦127865 they shut their¦127876 calves away in¦127873 the \add ≈stall\add*.
\v 11 Then¦127880 they¦127880 put Yahweh's¦127884 box¦127883 into the cart with the \add other\add* container with the gold¦127892 mice and¦127880 the replicas of their¦127895 tumours¦127895.
\v 12 \add Amazingly,\add* the cows¦127899 went¦127908 straight¦127897 down the road¦127903 going to Beyt-Shemesh. They \add ≈stayed on¦127900 that\add* one highway¦127906, walking and mooing, and they \add ≈never once deviated to the left or the right¦127914\add*, and the Philistine rulers¦127916 followed them as far as the border¦127922 of Beyt-Shemesh.
\p
\v 13 \add ≈Meanwhile in\add* Beyt-Shemesh, \add the people\add* were harvesting¦127928 wheat¦127931 in the valley¦127932, and when they \add ≈looked up\add* and saw¦127937 the box¦127940, \add ≈they were very happy\add*.
\v 14 The cart came¦127945 into the field¦127948 belonging to Yehoshua and \add then the cows just\add* stood there. There was a large¦127957 stone¦127956 there, and \add @the people\add* split¦127958 \add the sides of\add* the wooden cart \add into pieces to start a fire\add*, then offered¦127966 the cows as a burnt¦127967 sacrifice to Yahweh¦127968.
\v 15 Then the Levites¦127971 lifted down Yahweh's¦127977 box¦127976 and the \add other\add* container that was with it with the gold¦127989 objects in it, and they placed them on the large¦127994 stone¦127993. Then the men of Beyt-Shemesh¦127998 offered¦127999 up \add more\add* burnt¦128000 offerings¦128000 and sacrificed sacrifices to Yahweh on that day¦128003.
\v 16 The five¦128007 Philistine rulers¦128008 watched \add all this from a distance before\add* returning¦128012 to Ekron¦128013 that same day¦128014.
\s1 The gold replicas
\p
\v 17 The gold¦128020 \add replicas\add* of tumours that¦128021 the Philistines¦128023 sent¦128022 back as a guilt¦128024 offering¦128024 to Yahweh¦128025 were for \add the five cities\add*: Ashdod¦128026, Gaza¦128029, Ashkelon¦128031, Gat¦128033, and¦128018 Ekron¦128035.
\v 18 And the gold mice were from the number¦128041 of all the Philistine cities \add ≈under\add* the five rulers¦128047, including¦128039 fortified¦128049 cities as \add ≈well\add* as the villages¦128051 in the open country¦128052, and as far as the large¦128056 stone that¦128057 they sat Yahweh's¦128062 box¦128061 on¦128066. (It's \add still\add* in Yehoshua's field¦128066 in Beyt-Shemesh until¦128063 this day.)
\rem /s1 The Ark Moved to Kiriath-Jearim; The Ark at Kiriath-jearim
\p
\v 19 \add ≈But\add* some Beyt-Shemesh¦128076 men looked¦128078 into Yahweh's¦128080 box¦128079, and¦128072 he killed seventy¦128083\f + \fr 6:19 \ft TC: The oldest Hebrew text has 50,070 men being killed here, making it hard to imagine how that many men kept clambering over dead bodies to look into the sacred chest. (But possibly we're not understanding the situation correctly?) We have followed later manuscripts here.\f* of them. The people mourned¦128088 because¦128077 Yahweh had \add ≈caused such devastation\add* among the people.
\s1 The sacred chest is taken to Kiryat-Yearim
\rem /s1 The Covenant Box at Kiriath Jearim
\p
\v 20 The Beyt-Shemesh¦128102 men said¦128098, “Who can stand¦128105 in front¦128106 of the face¦128106 of Yahweh¦128107, this holy God¦128108? And \add ≈where should we send the box to from here\add*?”
\v 21 \add ≈So\add* they¦128127 sent¦128118 messengers¦128119 to those living in Kiriat¦128125-Yearim, saying, “The Philistines¦128128 have returned Yahweh's¦128132 box¦128131. Come \add ≈over here and¦128118 get it\add*.”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 \add ≈So¦128138\add* the men from Kiriat¦128142-Yearim came¦128138 and lifted up Yahweh's¦128147 box¦128146 and brought¦128148 it into Abinadab's¦128153 house¦128152 on¦128154 the hill. They consecrated¦128159 his son Eleazar¦128157 to \add ≈take care of\add* \add *it\add*.\x + \xo 7:1: \xt 2Sam 6:2-4; 1Ch 13:5-7.\x*
\s1 Shemuel guides Yisrael
\rem /s1 Samuel Rules Israel; Samuel Subdues the Philistines at Mizpah; Samuel judge over Israel
\p
\v 2 The \add ≈sacred chest¦128170 ended up\add* staying in¦128171 Kiriat-Yearim for twenty¦128176 years \add ≈but¦128167 the Israelis¦128182 longed sadly\add* for Yahweh¦128184.
\rem /s1 Samuel Leads Israel to Victory; Samuel as Judge
\p
\v 3 Then Shemuel told all the \add ≈Israelis¦128194\add*, “If you all \add really want\add* to return to Yahweh, remove¦128206 the foreign¦128210 gods¦128209 and the Ashtorets¦128212 from \add ≈among¦128211 you\add*. Then \add ≈decide firmly to serve¦128218\add* Yahweh and \add ≈no other\add*, then he'll rescue¦128220 you from the Philistines' \add ≈oppression\add*.”
\v 4 \add ≈So¦128225 the Israelis¦128227\add* removed¦128225 the Baal¦128230 and Ashtoret¦128233 \add idols\add*, and \add began to\add* serve¦128234 Yahweh¦128237 only.
\p
\v 5 Then Shemuel¦128242 \add ≈called\add*, “Gather all Yisrael¦128248 to Mitspah, and¦128241 I'll pray to Yahweh¦128254 for you¦128251 all.”
\v 6 \add ≈So¦128256\add* they gathered¦128256 at Mitspah, and drew water¦128260 and poured¦128261 it out \add ≈in¦128278 front¦128263\add* of Yahweh, and they fasted¦128265 on¦128278 that day¦128266 and said¦128268 there¦128269, “We have sinned¦128270 against Yahweh.” And Shemuel¦128273 \add ≈guided\add* the \add ≈Israelis¦128277\add* at Mitspah.
\p
\v 7 \add ≈When\add* the Philistines heard that the \add ≈Israelis\add* had gathered¦128284 at Mitspah, the Philistine rulers¦128290 \add ≈decided to attack\add* Yisrael¦128287. \add ≈When the Israelis\add* heard \add <that\add*, they were afraid of the Philistines
\v 8 and¦128303 \add ≈begged\add* Shemuel, “Don't¦128310 \add ≈stop\add* crying¦128314 out to our¦128318 god¦128318 Yahweh¦128317, \add ≈so that\add* he'll rescue¦128319 us from the Philistines¦128321.”
\v 9 \add ≈So¦128323\add* Shemuel¦128324 got a \add ≈young\add* lamb¦128325 and offered it up—a whole¦128331 burnt¦128330 up offering to Yahweh. And \add *he\add* cried¦128333 out to Yahweh for Yisrael¦128339, and Yahweh answered¦128340 him¦128340.
\v 10 \add ≈While\add* Shemuel¦128344 was offering up that burnt¦128346 offering, the Philistines approached to battle¦128349 against Yisrael¦128350. \add ≈But¦128343\add* that day¦128357 Yahweh¦128352 \add ≈made an intense sound¦128354 like thunder¦128351\add* that confused¦128362 the Philistines and¦128343 \add ≈the Israelis were able to defeat¦128363 them\add*.
\v 11 The Israeli¦128369 men¦128368 \add ≈ran\add* out from Mitspah and¦128367 chased the Philistines¦128376—killing them \add ≈all the way to\add* Beyt-Kar.
\p
\v 12 Shemuel¦128385 took¦128384 a single rock¦128386 and stood it between Mitspah and Shen¦128393. He \add ≈named\add* it ‘Eben-Ezer’ \add (which means ‘stone¦128386 of help’)\add*, saying, “Yahweh¦128405 helped¦128404 us \add ≈all the way up to\add* here.”
\v 13 \add ≈So¦128407\add* the Philistines \add ≈ended up being\add* subdued¦128407 and didn't¦128409 invade¦128413 Yisrael's¦128415 border again, and Yahweh¦128419 \add ≈worked\add* against the Philistines \add ≈as long as Shemuel lived\add*.
\v 14 The cities¦128426 that¦128427 the Philistines had taken¦128428 from Ekron¦128435 and as far as Gat¦128438 were returned to Yisrael. And \add so\add* Yisrael rescued their¦128441 territory¦128441 from the Philistines, and there was peace¦128447 between Yisrael and the Amorites¦128451.
\p
\v 15 \add ≈So¦128453\add* Shemuel¦128454 guided Yisrael¦128457 \add ≈while he lived\add*.
\v 16 \add ≈Every year\add* he \add ≈did the circuit¦128466\add* around Beyt-El, Gilgal¦128470, and Mitspah, and he \add ≈helped settled disputes for the people in¦128465\add* all those places¦128479,
\v 17 \add ≈but¦128482\add* he \add always\add* returned¦128482 to Ramah¦128483 because¦128484 his home¦128487 was there. He¦128492 would \add ≈judge¦128489 disputes\add* there, and¦128482 built¦128493 an altar¦128496 to Yahweh¦128497 there.
\c 8
\s1 The Israelis request a king
\rem /s1 The People Ask for a King; Israel Requests a King; Establishment of the monarchy; Israel Asks for a King; Israel Demands a King
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 As Shemuel¦128503 grew older, he¦128502 appointed his \add two\add* sons¦128507 as judges¦128508 over Yisrael¦128509.
\v 2 The \add ≈eldest\add* was Yoel¦128516 and¦128517 \add ≈his brother\add* was Abiyah¦128519. They \add ≈were based\add* in¦128521 Be'er-Sheva.
\v 3 \add ≈However,\add* they \add ≈didn't¦128524 take after¦128531 time, but¦128524 rather chased dishonest\add* gain—accepting bribes¦128535 and¦128524 perverting¦128536 justice¦128537.
\p
\v 4 \add ≈Eventually,\add* the Israeli¦128542 elders¦128542 met together¦128540 with Shemuel at Ramah¦128548
\v 5 and \add ≈requested\add*, “Listen¦128552, you're old \add now\add*, and your sons¦128555 don't¦128556 \add ≈follow your example\add*. \add ≈So¦128550\add* appoint¦128560 a king¦128563 for us now to \add ≈lead\add* us like all the other nations¦128567.”\x + \xo 8:5: \xt Deu 17:14.\x*
\v 6 \add ≈But¦128569\add* Shemuel¦128572 \add ≈thought that their request for a king¦128578 was an evil request, so\add* \add *he\add* prayed¦128580 to Yahweh¦128584
\v 7 \add ≈who replied\add*, “Listen to \add ≈everything that the people¦128594 are saying\add*, because¦128600 they're not rejecting \em you\em*, but¦128587 they're rejecting me from reigning¦128608 over them.
\v 8 They're doing¦128631 to you just what they've done¦128616 to me every since I \add ≈rescued\add* them out of Egypt¦128620 and right up until today: deserting¦128625 me and serving¦128626 other¦128628 gods¦128627.
\v 9 \add ≈So¦128636\add* listen¦128637 to \add ≈what they're saying\add* now, but¦128636 certainly¦128642 warn them about \add ≈what kings require of their¦128638 subjects\add*.”
\s1 Advance warnings about having a king
