\v 32 \add *They\add* rushed¦131867 greedily at the plunder, grabbing sheep¦131875, cattle¦131876, and calves which they¦131874 slaughtered¦131879 \add ≈right there\add* and ate¦131882 with the blood¦131886 \add still in them\add*.
\v 33 Then¦131888 King Sha'ul¦131889 was told, “Look, the people are sinning¦131893 against Yahweh¦131894 by eating¦131895 \add meat\add* with the blood¦131898 \add still in it\add*.”
\p “You've all acted¦131900 treacherously¦131900,” he \add ≈responded¦131899\add*, “\add ≈Now,\add* roll a big stone¦131905 \add over\add* to me.”\x + \xo 14:33: \xt Gen 9:4; Lev 7:26-27; 17:10-14; 19:26; Deu 12:16,23; 15:23.\x*
\v 34 Then¦131908 \add *he\add* said¦131908, “Go¦131909 out among¦131911 the \add ≈warriors\add* and tell them to bring¦131914 their \add ≈animals over here\add* to slaughter and to eat¦131922, so \add ≈they\add* don't¦131923 sin¦131925 against Yahweh¦131926 by eating¦131927 \add meat with¦131911\add* the blood¦131930 \add still in¦131911 it\add*.” So¦131908 the people brought¦131931 their \add ≈animals over\add* and slaughtered them there¦131941.
\v 35 Then¦131943 Sha'ul¦131944 built an altar to Yahweh¦131946—\add ≈it was the first one he¦131948 built\add*.
\rem /s1 Jonathan in Danger of Death
\p
\v 36 Then Sha'ul¦131955 said, “Let's go¦131956 after¦131957 the Philistines¦131958 tonight and plunder¦131961 them until¦131964 the light¦131966 of morning¦131967. Let's not leave¦131970 \add ≈any of them alive\add*.”
\p “Do \add ≈what you feel is right\add*,” they \add ≈responded¦131954\add*.
\p \add ≈But¦131954\add* the priest¦131981 said, “Let's \add ≈ask God¦131986 here first\add*.”
\p
\v 37 \add ≈So¦131988\add* Sha'ul¦131989 asked God¦131990, “Should \add #we\add* go¦131991 after¦131992 the Philistines¦131993? Will you¦131994 give¦131994 Yisrael¦131996 \add ≈victory over them\add*?” \add ≈But¦131988\add* \add @Yahweh\add* didn't¦131997 answer¦131998 him¦131998 that day¦131999.
\v 38 Then¦132002 Sha'ul¦132003 \add ≈commanded\add*, “All \add ≈you leaders of the warriors. Gather\add* here \add ≈so we can find out\add* what sin¦132013 \add ≈was committed\add* today.
\v 39 As Yahweh¦132020 who saves¦132021 Yisrael¦132024 lives, even if it's my \add own\add* son Yonatan, \add whoever sinned today\add* will certainly die.” \add ≈But¦132034 the people¦132038 didn't¦132034 volunteer\add* \add any information\add*.
\v 40 “You all can be on one side, and my son Jonathan and I will be on \add ≈the other\add* side,” Sha'ul¦132060 said.
\p “Do \add ≈whatever feels right to you\add*,” the people¦132057 \add ≈replied\add*.
\p
\v 41 Then Sha'ul \add ≈asked\add* Yisrael's¦132072 god¦132071 Yahweh¦132070, “\add ≈Show us the truth\add*.” And Sha'ul and Yonatan were selected—not the people¦132078.\x + \xo 14:41: \xt Num 27:21; 1Sam 28:6.\x*
\v 42 Then¦132081 Sha'ul¦132082 \add ≈asked\add* \add again\add*, “\add ≈Choose\add* between me and¦132081 my son Yonatan.” And Yonatan was selected.
\v 43 Sha'ul¦132092 \add ≈demanded from\add* Yonatan, “Tell me what you've done¦132099.”
\p “Actually,” Yonatan \add ≈answered\add*, “I did taste¦132105 some honey¦132113 from the end¦132107 of my staff¦132108, so take me—I'm \add ≈ready\add* to die¦132115.”
\p
\v 44 “May God¦132122 do¦132121 \add ≈to me whatever he wants\add*,” Sha'ul¦132118 said. “because¦132125 you will certainly die, Yonatan.”
\p
\v 45 \add ≈But¦132131\add* the people \add stood up to\add* Sha'ul¦132135, “Should Yonatan die? \add ≈Wasn't it him who saved\add* Yisrael¦132144 \add today\add*? \add ≈We won't¦132168 stand for it\add*. As Yahweh¦132148 lives, \add ≈not even a hair on¦132144 his head¦132153 will be touched\add* because¦132155 he worked with¦132144 God¦132159 today.” And \add so\add* the people rescued Yonatan and¦132131 he \add ≈wasn't executed\add*.
\p
\v 46 Then Sha'ul¦132174 \add ≈stopped chasing¦132175\add* the Philistines \add ≈and went¦132178 home\add*, and the Philistines \add also\add* returned to their¦132179 place¦132179.
\s1 Sha'ul's kingdom, and his family details
\rem /s1 Saul's Reign and Family; Saul's Military Successes; Saul's Continuing Wars
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 47 \add ≈After\add* Sha'ul¦132181 had taken¦132182 on¦132193 the kingship of Yisrael¦132186, he fought¦132187 against their enemies from all around¦132188: against the Moabites, the Ammonites¦132197, the Edomites, the kings¦132199 of Zobah¦132200, and¦132181 against the Philistines¦132201. Wherever he turned¦132205, he punished \add his enemies\add*.
\v 48 He acted bravely and¦132208 \add ≈defeated¦132210\add* \add even\add* the Amalekites, \add always\add* rescuing¦132214 Yisrael¦132217 from those¦132219 who \add came in to\add* plunder¦132219 it.
\rem /s1 Saul's Family
\p
\v 49 Sha'ul's sons¦132223 were Yonatan, Ishvi¦132226, and Malki-Shua. His oldest daughter¦132232 was Merab¦132235, and the younger¦132237 one was Mikal.
\v 50 His wife's name was Ahinoam¦132243 (daughter¦132244 of Ahima'ats). His army commander¦132248 was Abner¦132251 (son of Sha'ul's uncle¦132255 Ner¦132254—
\v 51 Kish was Sha'uls father, and¦132258 Abner's¦132265 father Ner¦132262 was Abiel's¦132268 son).
\p
\v 52 The conflict with the Philistines¦132276 was intensive for all of Sha'uls life, \add ≈so\add* whenever he saw¦132280 a \add ≈good warrior\add* or any strong, young man¦132284, then he would \add ≈conscript\add* \add #them\add*.
\c 15
\s1 Sha'ul destroys the Amalekites
\rem /s1 Saul and David; Saul Defeats the Amalekites but Spares Their King; Saul Destroys the Amalekites; War against the Amalekites; The Lord Rejects Saul as King
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add One day\add* Shemuel said¦132295 to Sha'ul¦132299, “It was Yahweh¦132302 who sent¦132301 me to anoint¦132303 you as king¦132304 over his people¦132307 Yisrael¦132310 \add back then\add*, \add ≈so\add* now, listen¦132312 to \add ≈what he has to say\add*.\x + \xo 15:1: \xt 1Sam 10:1.\x*
\v 2 Commander Yahweh¦132320 says¦132319, ‘I've¦132322 noticed what \add ≈the Amalekites\add* did¦132326 to Yisrael¦132328 when they came¦132334 out of Egypt¦132335—\add ≈they attacked them\add* \add as they passed by\add*.\x + \xo 15:2: \xt Exo 17:8-14; Deu 25:17-19.\x*
\v 3 \add ≈So\add* go¦132338 now and attack¦132339 Amalek¦132342 and destroy¦132343 \add #them\add* completely without compassion¦132352, putting men and women, children and infants to death¦132354, along with \add their\add* cattle and sheep¦132366, camels¦132367 and donkeys¦132370.’ ”
\p
\v 4 \add ≈So\add* Sha'ul¦132374 summoned¦132373 the people¦132377 to Telaim¦132379 and counted them: two¦132380 hundred¦132380 thousand foot¦132382 soldiers¦132382 as well as ten¦132383 thousand \add ≈warriors\add* from Yehudah¦132388.
\v 5 Then¦132390 Sha'ul¦132391 \add ≈took them\add* to Amalek¦132395 city¦132394, and¦132396 \add #they\add* lay in wait in the valley¦132397.
\v 6 Then¦132399 \add *he\add* \add ≈warned\add* the Kenites, “\add ≈You all need to move out from among\add* the Amalekites, \add ≈so that you all won't be attacked along with¦132422 them\add*. \add ≈That's because you all were kind to our ancestors\add* when they came¦132422 out of Egypt¦132423.” \add ≈So¦132399\add* the Kenites \add ≈moved out from among the Amalekites\add*.
\p
\v 7 Then¦132429 Sha'ul¦132430 \add ≈slaughtered the Amalekites¦132433\add* from Havilah¦132434 to Shur¦132436 (near Egypt¦132441),
\v 8 and¦132451 \add #they\add* captured¦132443 King¦132449 Agag¦132446 alive¦132450 \add ≈but¦132443\add* killed everyone¦132455 else with swords¦132459.
\v 9 \add ≈However¦132462, as well as sparing\add* Agag¦132466, they \add ≈took\add* the best sheep¦132470 and¦132461 cattle¦132471
\s1 God rejects Sha'ul
\rem /s1 Saul Rejected as King; Saul Is Rejected as King; The Lord Rejects Saul
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 \add ≈Then\add* Yahweh¦132496 \add ≈told\add* Shemuel,
\v 11 “I regret¦132502 that I've¦132505 \add ≈appointed\add* Sha'ul¦132508 as king because¦132503 he's turned¦132512 \add ≈away from following¦132513\add* me and hasn't \add ≈obeyed my instructions¦132516\add*.” \add ≈That really upset\add* Shemuel¦132520 and he cried¦132521 out to Yahweh¦132524 all night¦132527.
\v 12 Early the next morning¦132533, Shemuel¦132530 \add ≈got up\add* to meet Sha'ul, \add ≈but¦132529 someone\add* told \add *him\add*, “Sha'ul went to Karmel¦132540, and wow, he set up a monument¦132544 to himself. Then¦132529 he turned¦132545 around and went to Gilgal¦132548.”
\v 13 \add ≈When¦132550\add* Shemuel¦132551 \add ≈caught up with\add* Sha'ul, Sha'ul told him, “May Yahweh bless¦132558 you¦132559. I've¦132561 \add ≈followed Yahweh's¦132560 instructions\add*.”
\p
\v 14 “Then¦132567 what's \add ≈that bleating of sheep¦132572 that I can hear\add*?” \add ≈asked\add* Shemuel¦132568. “And \add ≈I'm sure I can hear cattle¦132576 sounds¦132570 too\add*?”
\p
\v 15 “\add Ah,\add* \add ≈the warriors took\add* them from the Amalekites¦132583,” Sha'ul¦132582 \add ≈answered\add*. “They \add ≈wanted to save\add* the best of the sheep¦132591 and¦132581 the cattle¦132592 in order to sacrifice¦132594 to your¦132596 god¦132596 Yahweh¦132595. \add ≈But¦132581\add* we¦132600 completely destroyed¦132600 the rest.”
\p
\v 16 “Stop,” said Shemuel¦132604, “and let me tell you what Yahweh¦132614 \add ≈told me last\add* night¦132616.”
\p “\add ≈Go ahead\add*,” \add @Sha'ul\add* \add ≈responded¦132603\add*.
\p
\v 17 \add ≈So\add* Shemuel told \add him\add*, “Although you \add ≈consider yourself insignificant\add*, aren't¦132625 you the head of the tribes¦132632 of Yisrael? Yahweh¦132636 anointed¦132635 you as king¦132637 over Yisrael,
\v 18 then¦132642 sent¦132642 you¦132642 \add ≈off with¦132644 the task of completely destroying the sinful Amalekites¦132643—fighting¦132654 until¦132656 they¦132657 were all destroyed¦132657\add*.
\v 19 \add ≈So¦132660\add* why didn't¦132661 you \add ≈obey Yahweh's¦132665 command\add*? \add ≈Instead,\add* you \add ≈headed straight for the plunder¦132669 and¦132660 disobeyed Yahweh\add*.”
\p
\v 20 “I \add ≈did listen¦132682 to Yahweh's¦132684 instructions\add*,” Sha'ul¦132677 \add ≈insisted\add*. “and \add ≈I did what he asked\add*. \add ≈So now\add* I've brought¦132691 Amalekite King¦132695 Agag¦132694 \add here\add* and I've completely destroyed¦132700 the Amalekites.
\v 21 \add Yes,\add* the people¦132703 \add ≈kept some of the best\add* sheep¦132705 and¦132706 cattle¦132706 to sacrifice¦132709 to your¦132711 god¦132711 Yahweh¦132710 here at¦132712 Gilgal¦132712.”
\p
\v 22 \add ≈But¦132714\add* Shemuel¦132715 \add ≈asked\add*,
\q1 “Is Yahweh¦132717 \add ≈happier\add* with burnt¦132718 offerings¦132718 and¦132719 sacrifices
\q1 than \add ≈with those who follow his instructions\add*?
\q1 Listen, \add ≈obedience\add* is better than sacrifice—
\q1 ≈paying attention¦132727 is \add worth\add* more than the fat of rams.
\q1
\v 23 Rebellion is as sinful as doing sorcery,
\q1 ≈and being stubborn is as sinful as worshipping idols.
\q1 Because you have rejected Yahweh's¦132744 \add ≈instructions\add*,
\q1 ≈he has rejected you as king¦132746.”
\rem /s1 Saul Pleads for Forgiveness
\p
\v 24 “\add Yes,\add* I've sinned¦132754,” Sha'ul¦132750 \add ≈responded¦132749\add*. “\add I can see that\add* I've \add ≈disobeyed Yahweh's¦132762 instruction, and yours too\add*, because¦132755 I feared¦132767 the people¦132770 and \add ≈did what they were requesting\add*.
\v 25 \add ≈But¦132774\add* now, please¦132776 forgive¦132775 my sin¦132779 and¦132774 come back with me so I¦132777 can worship¦132782 Yahweh¦132783.”
\p
\v 26 “\add No,\add* I won't¦132790 return¦132791 with you.” Shemuel¦132786 \add ≈replied\add*. “You've rejected Yahweh's¦132798 \add ≈instructions\add*, and¦132785 Yahweh has \add already\add* rejected you as Yisrael's¦132805 king¦132802.”
\p
\v 27 \add ≈As\add* Shemuel¦132809 turned¦132808 around to go¦132810, \add @Sha'ul\add* grabbed the edge of his robe¦132814 and it¦132815 tore.\x + \xo 15:27-28: \xt 1Sam 28:17; 1Ki 11:30-31.\x*
\v 28 “Today, Yahweh¦132821 has torn the kingdom of Yisrael¦132825 away from you,” Shemuel¦132819 told him. “And he's given¦132828 it to \add ≈another man who's\add* better¦132830 than you.
\v 29 And \add ≈by the way\add*, Yisrael's¦132835 powerful \add God\add* doesn't \add ≈go back on his word\add*, and¦132838 doesn't change¦132844 his mind \add ≈like people tend to do\add*.
\p
\v 30 “\add It's true that\add* I've¦132847 sinned¦132847,” Sha'ul said¦132846, “\add but¦132846\add* please¦132850 honour¦132849 me now in front of the Israeli people¦132854 and¦132846 their elders¦132852. And then¦132846 return¦132857 with me, so I can worship¦132859 your¦132861 god¦132861 Yahweh¦132860.”
\v 31 \add ≈So¦132863\add* Shemuel¦132864 followed Sha'ul back, and¦132867 Sha'ul worshipped¦132867 Yahweh¦132869.
\rem /s1 Samuel Executes King Agag
\p
\v 32 Then Shemuel¦132873 \add ≈commanded\add*, “Bring the Amalekite King¦132879 Agag¦132878 over here to me.”
\p \add ≈So\add* they \add brought\add* him over, and trembling,\f + \fr 15:32 \ft The meaning of the original word here is unclear and so there'll be different interpretations.\f* he said, “\add ≈I've faced up to death¦132891 now\add*.”\f + \fr 15:32 \ft Some translations interpret the original words as Agag thinking (or saying to himself) that he was going to be spared, but he surely knew Shemuel.\f*
\p
\v 33 Then Shemuel¦132906 told \add him\add*,
\q1 “Just as your sword¦132899 has made women childless,
\q1 so \add too\add* will your mother¦132904 be made childless among¦132903 women.”
\m And Samuel¦132895 hacked¦132905 Agag¦132909 to pieces¦132905 \add ≈in¦132912 front¦132910\add* of Yahweh¦132911 \add there\add* at¦132912 Gilgal¦132912.
\p
\v 34 Then¦132924 Shemuel¦132916 went \add home\add* to Ramah¦132917, and Sha'ul¦132918 returned to his house¦132922 in Gibeah¦132923.
\v 35 \add After that,\add* Shemuel¦132929 never saw Sha'ul again, but¦132926 he mourned¦132940 for him, and¦132926 Yahweh¦132945 regretted¦132946 that he'd made¦132949 Sha'ul king¦132949 over Yisrael¦132955.
\c 16
\s1 Shemuel anoints David as King
\rem /s1 David Anointed as King; Samuel Anoints David as King; Samuel Anoints David; David Is Anointed King
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦132959 spoke to Shemuel¦132962, “\add ≈How long are you going to keep mourning¦132967 for Sha'ul¦132970 for\add*, when I myself have rejected¦132972 him as king over Yisrael¦132976? Fill your horn with¦132991 oil and go. I'll¦132981 send you to Yishay¦132984 (Jesse) from Bethlehem, because¦132988 I've¦132990 seen \add ≈one of his sons who'd make a\add* \add good\add* king for me.”
\p
\v 2 “How can I \add ≈do that\add*?” \add ≈asked\add* Shemuel¦132996. “Sha'ul¦133000 would kill¦133001 me if he heard about it.”
\p “Take a heifer¦133005 \add ≈with¦133008 you\add*,” Yahweh said, “and¦132995 say, ‘I've¦133012 come¦133012 to sacrifice¦133010 to Yahweh.’
\v 3 \add ≈Invite\add* Yishay¦133015 to the sacrifice¦133016, and I'll¦133028 show you what do¦133022 to \add ≈from there\add*, and you should anoint¦133023 the one I tell you to.”
\p
\v 4 \add ≈So¦133031\add* Shemuel did what Yahweh¦133036 had told him, and went¦133037 to Bethlehem. The town¦133042 elders¦133041 trembled¦133040 when they met him, and \add ≈asked\add*, “Do you come¦133046 in peace¦133045?”
\p
\v 5 “\add Yes,\add* in¦133057 peace¦133050,” he \add ≈answered\add* “I've¦133053 come to sacrifice to Yahweh¦133052. Purify yourselves¦133054 and come with¦133057 me to the sacrifice.” Then¦133048 he purified Yishay¦133061 and his sons¦133064 and called¦133065 them to the sacrifice.
\p
\v 6 \add ≈When¦133069 they¦133070 arrived\add*, \add @Shemuel\add* saw¦133071 Eliab¦133074 and¦133069 \add ≈thought\add*, “Surely \add ≈this man's the one that Yahweh¦133078 wants to anoint¦133079\add*.”
\v 7 \add ≈But¦133081\add* Yahweh told \add *him\add*, “Don't¦133086 look¦133088 at his height¦133094 or outward¦133107 appearance¦133091, because¦133096 I've¦133097 rejected¦133097 him. Because it isn't¦133086 what a person sees with their eyes¦133107, but¦133081 Yahweh sees \add ≈their motives and¦133081 values\add*.”
\p
\v 8 Then¦133112 Yishay¦133113 called¦133112 for Abinadab¦133116 to \add ≈walk across in¦133123 front¦133118\add* of Shemuel¦133119, \add ≈but¦133112\add* he said¦133120 again, “Yahweh¦133127 hasn't chosen¦133126 that one.”
\v 9 Then¦133129 Yishay¦133130 made¦133129 Shammah¦133131 \add ≈walk across in¦133135 front\add*, \add ≈but¦133129\add* again he¦133138 said¦133132, “Yahweh¦133139 hasn't chosen¦133138 that one.”
\v 10 \add ≈So¦133141\add* \add all up,\add* Yishay made¦133141 seven¦133143 of his sons¦133144 \add ≈walk across in¦133156 front¦133145\add* of Shemuel, \add ≈but¦133141\add* Shemuel told \add *him\add*, “Yahweh¦133155 hasn't chosen¦133154 any of them.”
\v 11 Then¦133158 Shemuel¦133159 \add ≈asked\add* Yishay, “\add ≈Is this all your sons¦133164\add*?”
\p “\add Well,\add* there's just the youngest¦133168,” he \add ≈answered\add* “\add ≈but¦133158\add* you see, he's \add out\add* \add ≈looking after\add* the flock.”\f + \fr 16:11 \ft We deliberately left the word ‘flock’ here (rather than ‘sheep’), because¦133179 although young David is always thought of as ‘the shepherd boy’ in English culture, in the middle-East, \+bd goats\+bd* are probably a more important part of the flock than sheep.\f*
\p “Send for him,” Shemuel \add ≈instructed\add* \add *him\add*, “because we won't¦133180 \add ≈do anything else until¦133183 he gets\add* here.”
\v 12 \add ≈So¦133188\add* he sent¦133188 for him and brought¦133189 him in. He was a good-looker with beautiful¦133194 eyes¦133195 and red colouring, and Yahweh¦133200 said¦133199, “\add ≈Stand up\add* \add and\add* anoint¦133202 him, because¦133203 \add ≈he's the one\add*.”
\v 13 \add ≈So¦133208\add* Shemuel took¦133208 the horn \add full\add* of \add olive\add* oil¦133213 and anointed¦133214 him \add ≈right there with¦133216\add* his brothers¦133217, and Yahweh's¦133221 spirit¦133219 rushed¦133218 onto David¦133224 from that day¦133225 onwards¦133227. Then¦133208 Shemuel left and went¦133230 \add home\add* to Ramah¦133231.
\s1 Sha'ul employs David
\rem /s1 David in Saul's Court; David Serves in Saul's Court; David in Saul's Service; David Plays the Lyre for Saul
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 14 \add Meanwhile,\add* Yahweh's¦133235 spirit left Sha'ul¦133238, and \add instead\add* an evil spirit from Yahweh tormented¦133239 him,
\v 15 and¦133246 \add *his\add* servants¦133247 told him, “Look¦133249, an evil¦133257 spirit¦133254 from God¦133256 is tormenting¦133258 you¦133258.
\v 16 Let our master¦133263 please¦133262 \add ≈get your servants¦133264\add* to find a man who knows¦133268 how to play the harp. \add ≈Then, whenever\add* the evil¦133277 spirit¦133274 from God¦133276 is on¦133270 you, then he'll play \add ≈it\add* and it'll be good¦133280 for you.”
\p
\v 17 “\add Okay,\add*” Sha'ul¦133285 \add ≈responded¦133284\add*, “Find a man for me who's good at playing \add <it\add*, and bring¦133296 him¦133296 to me.”
\p
\v 18 Then¦133299 one of his servants \add ≈spoke up\add*, “Listen, I've¦133304 \add ≈noticed one of the sons\add* of Yishay¦133306 \add ≈in Bethlehem\add* who knows¦133309 how to play. \add He's also\add* \add ≈a very powerful warrior\add*, as well as being \add ≈good looking\add* and¦133299 \add ≈wise in what he says\add*. And Yahweh¦133319 is with him.”
\p
\v 19 \add ≈So¦133322\add* Sha'ul¦133323 sent¦133322 messengers¦133324 to Yishay¦133327 \add ≈to tell him\add*, “Your¦133331 son David¦133333 who looks after your¦133334 flock¦133336, send¦133329 him to me.”
\v 20 \add ≈So¦133338\add* Yishay¦133339 loaded a donkey¦133340 with bread¦133341, a skin of wine, and a young¦133344 goat¦133344, and sent¦133347 them to \add King\add* Sha'ul¦133354 with his son David¦133350.
\v 21 \add ≈When¦133360\add* David¦133357 got to \add *the king\add*, \add ≈he became his servant\add*, and¦133356 \add ≈he was liked so much that he was appointed as the one who carried the king's equipment\add*.
\v 22 Sha'ul¦133372 sent¦133371 \add a message back\add* to Yishay¦133375, saying, “Let David¦133380 \add ≈remain in¦133386 my service\add*, because¦133382 \add ≈I really like him¦133377\add*.”
\v 23 Then¦133388 whenever the spirit from God¦133392 \add ≈tormented\add* Sha'ul, David¦133397 would take the harp and play it. \add ≈That would bring\add* relief¦133403 to Sha'ul and \add ≈make him feel better\add*, and the evil spirit would \add ≈leave\add* him.
\c 17
\s1 Goliat Challenges the Israelis
\rem /s1 Goliath Challenges the Israelites; David and Goliath
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add At that¦133421 time,\add* the Philistines¦133414 gathered their¦133417 \add army\add* divisions together ready for battle¦133418. They assembled at¦133430 Sokoh in Yehudah¦133422, and camped¦133423 between Sokoh and Azekah¦133429 in Efes-Dammim.
\v 2 Sha'ul¦133433 and the Israeli¦133436 \add ≈warriors¦133434\add* gathered¦133437 and camped¦133438 in¦133439 the Elah¦133440 valley¦133439, then¦133433 they¦133437 arranged themselves for battle¦133442 against the Philistines¦133444.
\v 3 \add ≈So¦133446\add* the Philistines¦133446 stood on one hill and¦133446 \add ≈the Israelis¦133452\add* on the \add opposite\add* hill, with the valley¦133458 between¦133459 them.
\p
\v 4 Then¦133461 the Philistines¦133466 \add ≈sent a champion¦133464\add* out from their camp \add to represent them\add*. His name¦133468 was Goliat¦133467 and he came from Gat¦133469, and he was almost three metres¦133472\f + \fr 17:4 \ft Some Greek sources have a height closer to two metres.\f* \add ≈tall\add*.
\v 5 He \add ≈wore\add* a bronze helmet¦133475, and¦133475 his body armour had \add ≈overlapping plates\add* weighing¦133484 \add a total of\add* some fifty-five kilograms.
\v 6 He had bronze armour¦133492 on his legs, and¦133497 a bronze plate\f + \fr 17:6b \ft It's not totally clear what's being described in the second part of this sentence, so other interpretations might differ.\f* between¦133499 his shoulders¦133500.
\v 7 The wooden \add shaft¦133502 of\add* his spear was like a weaver's¦133506 beam and¦133502 his spear time weighed around seven kilograms. His¦133517 shield-bearer walked in front¦133517 of him¦133517.
\v 8 \add @Goliat\add* stood¦133519 \add there\add* and called¦133520 out to the Israeli¦133523 \add ≈warriors\add*, “Why have you all come out to \add ≈line up\add* for battle? \add Now,\add* \add ≈I'm\add* \add down here as\add* a Philistine¦133533, and all of you are Sha'ul's slaves, \add so\add* choose a man¦133540 for yourselves and let him come down \add here to fight\add* me.
\v 9 If he's able to fight¦133547 with me and \add ≈kill\add* me, then we'll become your slaves. \add ≈But¦133549 if I'm the winner\add* and kill him, then you'll all become our slaves and \add ≈work for\add* us.”
\v 10 Then¦133566 \add *he said¦133566\add*, “I personally scoff at Yisrael's¦133573 lines¦133572 today. Give me a man¦133579 so we¦133580 can fight¦133580 together¦133581.”
\v 11 When¦133583 Sha'ul¦133584 and the Israelis heard \add all\add* \add *that\add*, they¦133594 were discouraged¦133593 and very afraid.
\s1 David and Goliat
\rem /s1 Jesse Sends David to Saul's Camp; David in Saul's Camp
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 12 \f + \fr 17:12 \ft Verses 12–31 aren't included in every ancient Greek translation.\f*\add ≈Now\add* David¦133598 was the son of Yishay¦133608 (from the Efrat clan, he lived in Bethlehem in Yehudah¦133606) who had eight¦133610 sons¦133611, and by Sha'ul's time¦133613, was quite old compared to other men¦133617.\f + \fr 17:12 \ft There's a small variation in the original manuscripts at the end of the verse, but it has minimal effect on the essential story-line.\f*
\v 13 Yishay's¦133623 three oldest sons (Eliab¦133637, Abinadab¦133640, and¦133619 Shammah¦133642) had gone to the battle with¦133636 Sha'ul¦133628,
\v 14 \add ≈but¦133644\add* David¦133644 was the youngest¦133646. \add ≈While\add* the three¦133647 oldest \add ≈were\add* with Sha'ul¦133651,
\v 15 David¦133654 \add ≈went back and forth between\add* Sha'ul\add 's camp\add* and looking after his father's¦133663 flock¦133662 at Bethlehem.
\p
\v 16 For forty¦133673 days, the Philistine¦133669 \add champion\add* came¦133668 out and presented himself in the early¦133670 morning and \add again\add* in the evening¦133671.
\p
\v 17 \add One day\add* Yishay¦133678 said to his son David¦133679, “\add ≈Here's a sack\add* of roasted¦133686 grain¦133686 and ten loaves of bread¦133689. Take them straight away to your brothers at the camp.
\v 18 And take¦133712 these¦133699 ten¦133696 cuts of cheese¦133698 to the commander¦133701 of \add ≈their¦133711 unit\add*. \add ≈Find out how\add* your¦133706 brothers¦133706 \add ≈are doing\add* and¦133695 bring¦133700 back \add ≈anything they want to send to me\add*.
\v 19 Sha'ul¦133714 and them and the \add ≈other warriors\add* are in¦133720 the Elah¦133719 valley¦133720, fighting¦133722 against the Philistines¦133725.”
\p
\v 20 \add ≈So¦133727\add* David¦133728 got up early¦133727 in¦133729 the morning¦133729 and left the flock¦133733 with¦133729 a \add ≈shepherd\add*, and set out just like Yishay¦133741 had told¦133740 him to. \add ≈When¦133731\add* he got to the battleground and the camp¦133743, the \add ≈warriors\add* were just going¦133745 out to the battle line and they were shouting¦133749 \add ≈the battle cry¦133749\add*.
\v 21 The Israelis and¦133754 the Philistines¦133754 arranged \add themselves\add*—battle line to battle line.
\v 22 David¦133760 left \add ≈what he'd brought\add* with a \add ≈guard\add*, and ran¦133770 into the battle¦133771 line¦133771. Then¦133759 he \add ≈caught up with\add* his brothers¦133774 and asked¦133773 \add ≈them how they were doing\add*.
\v 23 \add ≈While¦133792\add* he was talking¦133779 with them, look, the Philistine¦133786 \add ≈champion¦133783\add* from Gat¦133788 was coming¦133784 out \add ≈with his challenge\add*. Goliat¦133785 spoke¦133793 \add ≈like he had before\add* and¦133777 David¦133797 \add ≈heard it all\add*.
\v 24 \add ≈But¦133799 when\add* the Israelis¦133801 saw¦133802 the \add ≈champion\add*, they¦133802 were \add ≈terrified\add* and¦133799 fled¦133806 \add ≈away from him¦133807\add*.
\v 25 saying \add to each other\add*, “Have you seen this man who's coming out? He's certainly coming to mock Yisrael¦133814! Any man who can defeat him will be made very wealthy¦133830 by the king¦133831, and¦133811 he'll give his daughter¦133837 to him, plus¦133825 \add ≈his extended family will be exempt from paying taxes\add*.”
\p
\v 26 “What \add was it that'll\add* be done¦133858 for the man¦133859 who \add ≈kills\add* this Philistine and¦133848 take's Yisrael's¦133869 disgrace away?” David¦133849 asked some of the men¦133852 standing¦133853 around him. “Because who does that uncircumcised¦133873 Philistine \add think he is\add* that he would taunt the \add ≈army\add* of the living¦133879 God¦133878?”
\v 27 Then¦133881 the \add ≈men\add* told him \add ≈again\add* what would be given to the man¦133889 who \add ≈defeats\add* him.
\p
\v 28 \add ≈But¦133893 when\add* David's¦133905 oldest¦133896 brother Eliab¦133894 heard him talking to the men¦133900, \add ≈he got very angry\add* and \add ≈scolded\add* him, “Why have you come down here? Who's looking after those¦133918 few sheep¦133917 that you left in¦133897 the desert? I know you \add ≈have a big head\add* and \add ≈you're just a troublemaker\add*—you \add just\add* want to watch the battle¦133931.”
\p
\v 29 “What have I done¦133937 now?” David¦133935 \add ≈asked\add*. “Wasn't it \add ≈just a question\add*?”
\v 30 Then¦133943 he went over to another¦133948 \add group\add* and \add ≈asked the same question and got the same answer¦133955\add*.
\p
\v 31 \add ≈So¦133958 it got around what David¦133963 was saying, and¦133958 when Sha'ul¦133967 heard¦133958 about it\add*, he sent¦133968 for him.
\rem /s1 David Kills Goliath
\v 32 David¦133971 told \add *the king\add*, “Don't¦133975 \add ≈be discouraged\add*. Your¦133974 servant¦133982 will go¦133983 and¦133984 fight¦133984 against this Philistine¦133987.”
\p
\v 33 “You can't¦133995 go¦133997 against¦133998 this Philistine¦134000 and¦133990 fight¦134002 him,” Sha'ul¦133991 told David¦133994. “You're \add still\add* a lad, but¦133990 he's been a \add ≈professional warrior since¦134011 he was young\add*.”
\p
\v 34 “Your servant¦134021 has been \add working\add* for his father¦134022 tending the flock,” David¦134015 \add ≈replied\add*. “\add Sometimes\add* a lion¦134025 or a bear has come and \add ≈taken\add* a sheep¦134030 from the flock
\v 35 and I would go after¦134034 it and \add ≈beat it\add* and rescue¦134036 \add <the sheep\add* from its mouth¦134037. If it \add ≈went¦134033 to attack\add* me, then¦134033 I'd grasp it by the jaw and beat it and kill¦134043 it.
\v 36 Your¦134046 servant¦134053 has \add ≈killed\add* both a lion¦134048 and a bear, and that uncircumcised¦134056 Philistine¦134055 will \add ≈end up\add* \add just\add* like them, because¦134060 he's insulted the \add ≈army\add* of the living¦134064 God¦134063.”
\v 37 Then \add *he\add* added, “Yahweh who has saved me from the lion¦134073 and from the bear, \em he\em* will \add be the one to\add* save me from that Philistine¦134079.”
\p “Go \add then\add*, and Yahweh be with you.” Sha'ul¦134083 \add ≈assented\add*.
\v 38 Then¦134092 Sha'ul¦134093 \add had\add* David¦134096 dressed in his \add own battle\add* attire, and¦134092 then¦134092 in body¦134106 armour¦134106 with a bronze¦134100 helmet¦134099.
\v 39 David¦134109 strapped¦134108 his sword¦134112 over \add ≈the top\add* and¦134108 then¦134108 tried¦134115 to walk because¦134117 he hadn't been trained \add in wearing it\add*. \add ≈But¦134108\add* \add *he\add* told Sha'ul¦134125, “I \add ≈couldn't walk in all that without practice\add*,” \add ≈so\add* he took¦134133 it \add all\add* off.
\v 40 Then¦134137 he \add ≈picked up\add* his staff¦134138, and selected five¦134143 smooth¦134144 stones¦134146 from the riverbed¦134150. He¦134152 placed them in¦134153 the pouch¦134158 of his shepherd's¦134154 bag, and \add ≈picked up\add* his sling¦134159 and \add ≈headed towards\add* the Philistine¦134164 \add champion\add*.
\s1 David fronts up to Goliat
\rem /s1 David Defeats Goliath
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 41 Then¦134166 \add @Goliat\add* came closer and¦134166 closer to David¦134172, with his¦134176 shield-bearer walking in front¦134176 of him¦134176.
\v 42 When¦134179 \add *he\add* looked¦134178 and¦134178 \add ≈realised¦134180\add* that David¦134183 was a reddish, good-looking lad, he despised¦134184 him
\v 43 and¦134195 \add ≈asked\add* \add *him\add*, “Are you coming¦134205 to me with¦134208 a stick¦134208 because you think I'm a dog?” Then¦134195 the Philistine cursed¦134209 David¦134199 by his gods¦134214.
\v 44 “Come¦134217 \add ≈over here\add*,” \add *he\add* told David¦134220, “and let me give¦134223 your¦134226 flesh¦134226 to the \add ≈vultures and wild animals¦134229\add*.”
\p
\v 45 “You're coming to me with¦134241 a sword¦134241 and spear¦134242 and dagger,” David¦134234 \add ≈shouted back\add*. “But¦134233 I'm coming to you in¦134241 the name¦134248 of commander¦134250 Yahweh¦134249, the god¦134251 of Yisrael's¦134253 \add ≈army¦134250\add* that you've¦134255 been taunting.
\v 46 Today, Yahweh¦134260 will \add ≈help me defeat you\add* and I'll knock you down and cut off your head¦134266. \add ≈What's more,\add* I'll give¦134268 the Philistine army corpses¦134269 to the \add ≈vultures and wild animals¦134276\add* today, then \add ≈everyone\add* will know¦134278 that there's¦134283 a god¦134284 in¦134261 Yisrael¦134285.
\v 47 \add ≈Everyone¦134300 here will learn\add* that Yahweh¦134298 doesn't save with¦134295 swords¦134295 or spears¦134296, because¦134292 \add *he's\add* in¦134295 charge of the battle¦134301 and¦134287 will \add ≈help us defeat\add* you¦134303 all.”
\p
\v 48 Then¦134306 as \add @Goliat\add* went¦134311 closer to meet David, David ran¦134317 quickly¦134315 towards \add *him\add* at the battle¦134318 line¦134318.
\v 49 \add *He\add* \add ≈slipped\add* his hand¦134326 into his bag and took out a single stone and slung¦134333 it towards the Philistine¦134337—striking him on¦134343 the forehead. The¦134323 stone sank into his forehead and he collapsed forwards onto the ground¦134348,
\v 50 and \add so\add* David¦134351 defeated the Philistine with a sling¦134355 and a stone¦134356—knocking him down and killing him. Not having a sword¦134362 with him,\x + \xo 17:50: \xt 2Sam 21:19.\x*
\v 51 David¦134369 ran¦134368 and stood¦134370 over the Philistine¦134373—drawing \add @Goliat's\add* sword¦134377 out of its sheath¦134379 to kill him¦134380 and cut¦134381 off his head¦134386.\x + \xo 17:51: \xt 2Sam 21:19.\x*
\rem /s1 Israel Routs the Philistines
\p When¦134368 the Philistines¦134388 saw¦134387 that their¦134392 powerful champion¦134392 was dead, they fled¦134393
\v 52 and the \add ≈Israeli¦134397 warriors¦134396 started yelling\add* and they¦134411 chased the Philistines into the valley¦134407 and as far as the gates¦134409 of Ekron. The \add ≈dead bodies\add* of the Philistines \add could be seen\add* on¦134414 the road all the way¦134414 from Shaaraim¦134415 to Gat¦134418 and Ekron.
\v 53 \add ≈When¦134430 the Israelis¦134425\add* returned¦134423 from chasing¦134426 the Philistines¦134428, they \add ≈raided the valuables\add* from their¦134432 camp.
\v 54 David¦134435 put \add @Goliat's\add* equipment into his \add own\add* tent¦134446, then¦134434 he carried his head¦134438 to Jerusalem.
\s1 Sha'ul asks about David
\rem /s1 David Is Presented to Saul
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 55 When Sha'ul¦134450 had seen David¦134453 going¦134454 out to meet \add @Goliat\add*, he'd \add ≈asked\add* Abner, the army commander¦134461, “Whose son is this lad, Abner?”
\p “As surely as you live, \add ≈your¦134474 majesty\add*,” \add ≈replied\add* Abner¦134460, “I don't know¦134478.”
\p
\v 56 “Find out whose son he is,” the king¦134481 \add ≈had commanded\add*.
\p
\v 57 Then¦134505 when David¦134493 had returned¦134492 from \add ≈killing¦134494\add* the Philistine¦134497, Abner¦134500 brought¦134501 him \add ≈to stand in front¦134502\add* of Sha'ul¦134503, and he was holding \add @Goliat's\add* head¦134504.
\v 58 “Whose son are you¦134514, lad?” Sha'ul¦134510 \add asked\add*.
\p “I'm the son of your¦134520 servant¦134520 Yishay¦134521, \add ≈who lives in Bethlehem\add*,” \add ≈replied\add* David¦134517.
\c 18
\s1 David's friendship with Yonatan
\rem /s1 Saul Becomes Jealous of David; Jonathan's Covenant with David; Saul's Jealousy of David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 After \add @David\add* had finished¦134526 speaking¦134527 to \add *the king\add*, him and¦134525 Yonatan \add ≈became close friends\add*, in¦134534 fact Yonatan \add ≈really\add* loved¦134536 him.
\v 2 \add From\add* that day¦134543, Sha'ul¦134542 kept David \add with him\add*, and¦134541 \add ≈wouldn't allow him to return¦134547 home\add*.
\v 3 Then¦134551 Yonatan and¦134551 David¦134553 \add ≈made an agreement¦134554 together\add*, because¦134555 \add @Yonatan\add* loved him as much as he loved himself.
\v 4 Yonatan¦134560 took off the cloak that¦134564 he was wearing and gave¦134566 it to David¦134567, along with his armour¦134568, his sword¦134571 and bow, and even his belt¦134577.
\v 5 David¦134580 went¦134579 \add ≈on¦134581 missions\add* wherever¦134582 Sha'ul sent¦134583 him and \add always\add* succeeded. Sha'ul \add ≈put him in¦134581 charge of the warriors¦134589\add*, and that pleased¦134591 all the people¦134595, as well as Sha'ul's servants¦134598.
\s1 Sha'ul becomes envious of David
\rem /s1 Saul Becomes Jealous of David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 6 When \add ≈they'd all\add* come back after killing¦134606 the Philistine¦134609, the women¦134611 from the Israeli¦134614 cities¦134614 had come out to meet King¦134621 Sha'ul¦134620 with singing¦134616 and dancing¦134618—playing tambourines¦134622 and \add ≈other instruments¦134624 with great happiness\add*.
\v 7 \add ≈However\add*, as they danced¦134628 they sang,\x + \xo 18:7: \xt 1Sam 21:11; 29:5.\x*
\q1 “Sha'ul¦134631 has \add ≈killed\add* his thousands,
\q1 ≈and¦134626 David¦134634 his tens of thousands.”
\m
\v 8 Sha'ul¦134638 \add ≈found that offensive and¦134637 it made him very angry\add*. “They've attributed tens of thousands to David¦134646,” he \add ≈complained\add*, “but¦134637 \add only\add* thousands to me. \add ≈Next thing he'll end up as king\add*.”
\v 9 \add ≈So¦134656\add* from that day¦134663 onwards¦134665, Sha'ul¦134657 \add ≈remained wary of David¦134662 and¦134665 kept an eye on him\add*.
\rem /s1 Saul Tries to Kill David
\p
\v 10 Then the next day, an evil spirit¦134671 from God¦134672 rushed¦134670 on¦134680 Sha'ul and \add even\add* in¦134680 the middle¦134680 of the house¦134682 he \add started\add* prophesying¦134679. As David¦134683 was playing¦134684 near him (as he did every day), Sha'ul was holding a spear¦134689
\v 11 and¦134694 he \add suddenly\add* hurled¦134694 \add *it\add*, saying, “I'll¦134700 \add ≈pin that David to\add* the wall¦134702.” \add ≈But¦134694\add* twice when he did that, David \add ≈managed to elude¦134703 him\add*.
\p
\v 12 \add ≈So¦134708\add* Sha'ul¦134709 \add ended up\add* afraid¦134708 of David¦134711 because¦134712 Yahweh¦134715 was with him, but¦134708 \add ≈was no longer with the king\add*.
\v 13 Then¦134721 Sha'ul¦134722 sent him \add ≈out of the room\add* and appointed¦134724 him as the commander¦134726 of a thousand¦134728 warriors, \add ≈so\add* he came¦134730 and went¦134729 in front¦134731 of the people¦134732.\f + \fr 18:13 \ft It's not entirely clear what the implications of this coming and going were (and similarly at the end of v16). It could easily refer to him returning successfully from military excursions.\f*
\v 14 Yahweh¦134742 was with David¦134736 and he was succeeding¦134741 in \add ≈everything he did\add*.
\v 15 Sha'ul¦134746 \add ≈knew about his¦134753 successes¦134750 and¦134752 couldn't stand looking at him¦134753\add*,
\v 16 \add ≈but¦134755 all the people of\add* Yisrael¦134757 and Yehudah¦134758 loved David¦134762 because¦134763 \add ≈they observed his comings¦134767 and goings¦134766\add*.
\s1 David wins Mikal as his wife
\rem /s1 David Marries Michal; David Marries Saul's Daughter
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 17 \add One day\add* Sha'ul told David¦134775, “Look, \add here's\add* my oldest daughter¦134777 Merab¦134779—I'll give¦134781 her to you for a wife. \add The only condition is that you\add* \add ≈become¦134786 a powerful warrior\add* for me and fight¦134792 Yahweh's¦134794 battles¦134793.” (Sha'ul had said \add to himself\add*, “\add ≈I don't¦134797 need to hurt him physically—I'll let the Philistines¦134807 do that\add* \add for me\add*.”)
\v 18 “Who am I,” David¦134811 \add ≈asked\add*, “and who are my relatives in my father's¦134820 clan in Yisrael¦134821, that I should become the king's son-in-law?”
\p
\v 19 \add ≈However,\add* when it was the time¦134829 for Sha'ul¦134836 to give his daughter¦134834 Merab¦134833 to David¦134837, she was given¦134839 to Adriel¦134840 the Meholathite¦134841 \add ≈to marry\add*.
\p
\v 20 \add ≈Now\add* Sha'ul's \add other\add* daughter¦134846 Mikal loved¦134844 David¦134851, and¦134844 \add when\add* they told \add *the king\add*, \add ≈he was pleased¦134854 about it\add*,
\v 21 \add ≈saying\add* \add to himself\add*, “I'll¦134860 give¦134860 her to him \add ≈so\add* she can become a trap for him \add ≈so\add* the Philistines¦134871 \add ≈could be the ones to attack\add* him.” \add ≈So¦134858\add* he said a second¦134877 time, “You'll become a son-in-law of mine today.”
\v 22 Then¦134882 Sha'ul¦134883 commanded¦134882 his servants, “Tell David¦134891 privately¦134892, ‘Listen, the king is pleased with¦134892 you, and¦134882 all of us his servants love you. \add ≈So¦134882\add* now \add we think that you should\add* become the king's¦134904 son-in-law.’ ”
\p
\v 23 \add ≈So¦134906\add* they¦134906 told David¦134910 privately¦134909, \add ≈but¦134906\add* \add *he\add* \add ≈responded¦134915\add*, “\add ≈Something that significant isn't likely to happen\add*. I'm \add ≈hard up\add* and¦134906 \add ≈without much honour\add*.”
\p
\v 24 \add ≈When¦134927\add* Sha'ul's¦134929 servants¦134928 told him what David¦134935 had said,
\v 25 he \add ≈replied\add*, “\add ≈Pass this onto\add* David, ‘The king \add ≈doesn't want an expensive\add* bride¦134948 price—only a hundred¦134950 Philistine foreskins¦134951 taken from the king's enemies¦134954.’ ” \add ≈But¦134938\add* Sha'ul¦134939 was \add still wanting\add* the Philistines¦134952 \add to be the ones\add* \add ≈to kill\add* David.
\v 26 \add ≈Now¦134976 when\add* David¦134968 \add ≈heard this from\add* the servants¦134967, \add ≈he decided that he could indeed marry the king's¦134978 daughter within the time¦134981 period that had been given\add*,
\v 27 \add ≈so\add* he and his men \add ≈got ready\add* and went and \add ≈killed¦134989\add* \em two\em* hundred¦134991 Philistine men. He brought¦134993 them to the king¦134999 and \add ≈had them counted to fulfill the pledge\add*, and so Sha'ul¦135005 \add had to\add* give his daughter¦135009 Mikal to become¦135000 his wife.
\p
\v 28 \add ≈But¦135013 when\add* Sha'ul¦135014 \add ≈realised¦135013\add* that Yahweh¦135017 was \add ≈helping\add* David¦135020, and that his daughter¦135022 loved¦135025 him¦135025,
\v 29 \add ≈he became more\add* afraid¦135029 of David, \add ≈so he remained hostile\add* to David \add ≈from then¦135027 on\add*.
\p
\v 30 \add ≈Whenever\add* the Philistine commanders \add ≈decided to attack\add*, David¦135052 was more successful¦135051 than \add ≈any other of Sha'ul's commanders, so he became very famous\add*.
\c 19
\s1 Sha'ul persecutes David
\rem /s1 Saul Tries to Kill David; Jonathan Intercedes for David; David Is Persecuted by Saul
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Sha'ul¦135062 \add ≈urged\add* his son Yonatan and all his servants¦135071 to \add ≈kill¦135072\add* David, \add ≈but¦135061\add* Yonatan \add ≈really liked\add* David
\v 2 and told \add *him\add*, “My father¦135090 Sha'ul¦135089 \add ≈wants to get rid of you¦135091, so please¦135095 watch out\add* in¦135096 the morning¦135096. \add ≈Find a hiding place¦135098 and stay¦135097 there\add*.
\v 3 And \add ≈what I'll do\add*, I'll go out and stand¦135103 by my father in¦135113 the countryside¦135107 where¦135108 you're¦135109 \add hiding\add*, and I'll \add ≈bring you into the conversation to see what he'll say about you\add*.”
\p
\v 4 \add ≈So\add* \add the next morning,\add* Yonatan spoke¦135123 well of David to his father¦135130 Sha'ul¦135129, saying, “Don't¦135133 let the king¦135136 sin¦135135 against his servant¦135137, against David, because¦135143 he hasn't sinned¦135141 against you, and actually, \add ≈he's been a big help\add* to you.
\v 5 He¦135158 \add ≈took his life¦135153 into his hands when he fought against\add* \add @Goliat\add*, and¦135150 Yahweh¦135160 \add ≈used it to save\add* all Yisrael¦135165. \add ≈So¦135150\add* why would you sin¦135169 against innocent¦135171 blood¦135170 by putting David¦135175 to death \add ≈for no reason\add*.”
\p
\v 6 Sha'ul¦135179 listened¦135178 to Yonatan, then¦135178 \add *he\add* promised, “As surely as Yahweh¦135186 lives, he won't be put to death¦135189.”
\v 7 \add Afterwards,\add* Yonatan called¦135191 David and told him \add ≈what had been said\add*. Then¦135191 he brought¦135203 David back \add to work for\add* Sha'ul¦135210 and \add ≈things carried on as before¦135212\add*.
\rem /s1 Michal Helps David Escape from Saul
\p
\v 8 Then war¦135218 \add ≈broke out\add* again, and David¦135221 went¦135220 out and battled against the Philistines¦135223 and defeated¦135224 them \add ≈so badly that\add* they¦135228 fled¦135228 away from \add ≈him¦135229\add*.
\p
\v 9 \add One day\add* as Sha'ul¦135238 was sitting¦135241 in his house¦135240, an evil spirit¦135232 from Yahweh¦135233 \add ≈came on¦135240 him\add*. He had his spear¦135242 in his hand, and¦135231 David¦135244 was playing¦135245 \add the harp\add*.
\v 10 Sha'ul¦135249 tried to hit David with¦135251 the spear, but¦135248 David eluded¦135254 him and the spear went into the wall. Then¦135248 David slipped away and fled that night¦135265.
\rem /s1 Michal Saves David's Life
\p
\v 11 Sha'ul¦135270 sent¦135269 messengers¦135271 to watch¦135276 David's¦135275 house¦135274 to \add ≈kill¦135277\add* him in¦135278 the morning¦135278, but¦135269 his wife Mikal told \add *him\add*, “If you don't¦135286 \add do something to\add* save your life¦135290 tonight, you'll be dead by tomorrow¦135292.”\x + \xo 19:11: \xt Psa 59 header.\x*
\v 12 \add ≈So¦135296\add* Mikal¦135297 lowered¦135296 David¦135300 out through¦135301 the window¦135302, and he was able to slip out and take off.
\v 13 Then¦135307 \add *she\add* took¦135307 a household idol¦135311 and put it in¦135322 the bed, putting a goats' hair¦135318 quilt¦135317 \add ≈where the head¦135320 should be\add* and covering¦135321 it with¦135322 the \add bed-\add*clothes.
\v 14 \add ≈When\add* Sha'ul¦135326 sent¦135325 messengers¦135327 to \add ≈arrest\add* David¦135331, she \add ≈told\add* \add <them\add*, “He's sick¦135333.”
\v 15 Sha'ul¦135338 sent¦135337 the messengers¦135341 \add back\add*, \add ≈telling\add* \add <them\add*, “Bring him up to me \add ≈bed¦135349 and all so I can kill¦135351 him\add*.”
\v 16 \add ≈But¦135353\add* when the messengers¦135354 \add ≈returned\add*, look, it was a household idol¦135356 in the bed¦135359 with a goats' hair¦135361 quilt¦135360 as its¦135362 head¦135362.
\v 17 Sha'ul \add had Mikal brought in and\add* \add ≈questioned\add*, “Why did you deceive¦135371 me like that and sent my enemy¦135375 off, so that he slipped away?”
\p “He \add ≈told me that he'd kill¦135388 me\add*,” Michal¦135368 \add ≈answered\add* Sha'ul, “\add ≈if I didn't help him escape¦135376\add*.”
\rem /s1 David Joins Samuel in Ramah
\p
\v 18 \add After\add* David¦135390 had slipped away, he went to Shemuel¦135411 in Ramah¦135397 and told him everything that Sha'ul¦135408 had done¦135405 to him, then¦135390 \add ≈the two of them\add* went and stayed in Nayot.
\v 19 \add ≈But¦135416\add* Sha'ul¦135417 was told that David¦135420 was in¦135421 Nayot¦135421 \add ≈near\add* Ramah¦135423,
\v 20 \add ≈so\add* \add *he\add* sent¦135425 messengers to \add ≈capture\add* David¦135431. \add ≈When¦135425\add* they saw¦135432 a group of prophets prophesying and Shemuel¦135438 standing¦135439 \add ≈as head\add* over them, God's spirit¦135447 came onto Sha'ul's messengers and they also prophesied.
\v 21 They¦135464 told Sha'ul¦135455 and he sent more messengers and they also prophesied. \add *He\add* sent messengers a third¦135467 time, and they \add started\add* prophesying as well.
\v 22 \add ≈Finally\add* Sha'ul \add himself\add* \add ≈headed towards\add* Ramah and got as far as the large¦135482 \add water\add* cistern¦135481 in Seku. He asked¦135485 \add ≈around about\add* Shemuel¦135488 and David¦135489, and \add ≈was told that¦135483\add* they were in Nayot \add ≈near\add* Ramah.
\v 23 \add ≈So¦135496\add* he went¦135515 to Nayot, and¦135496 God's spirit¦135508 came on¦135502 him as well. As he \add ≈walked\add*, he \add kept\add* prophesying¦135512 until¦135513 he entered Nayot.
\v 24 He took off his \add outer\add* clothes¦135524 and prophesied \add ≈in¦135546 front¦135529\add* of Shemuel. He lay \add ≈undressed\add* all that day¦135535 and all night¦135539, so \add that's why\add* people¦135543 ask, “Is Sha'ul¦135545 a prophet as well?”\x + \xo 19:24: \xt 1Sam 10:11-12.\x*
\c 20
\s1 Yonatan helps David
\rem /s1 The Friendship of David and Jonathan; Jonathan Helps David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then David¦135550 fled¦135549 from Nayot \add ≈near\add* Ramah¦135553 and went¦135554 to Yonatan and \add ≈asked\add*, “What have I done¦135560? What did I do wrong? What sin¦135566 \add ≈has made your father so angry\add* that he \add ≈wants to take\add* my life¦135573?”
\p
\v 2 “Far from it,” Yonatan \add ≈responded¦135575\add*. “You won't¦135578 die. Listen, my father doesn't do \add ≈anything, big or small¦135590, without telling me\add*. \add ≈So¦135575\add* why would my father hide this from me \add if it was his plan\add*? \add No,\add* \add ≈you're wrong\add*.”
\p
\v 3 “For sure, your father knows¦135612 that \add ≈you've taken a liking to me\add*,” said David, “and he \add ≈would have decided not to tell you so you wouldn't be upset\add*.” Then¦135607 David made an oath again¦135608, “Indeed, as Yahweh¦135632 is alive, and as your soul¦135634 is alive, death¦135639 \add ≈is definitely only a step away from me\add*.”
\p
\v 4 “I'll¦135650 do¦135650 anything for you—whatever¦135646 you say¦135648,” Yonatan \add ≈confirmed\add*.
\p
\v 5 “Listen, the new moon¦135662 \add celebration\add* is tomorrow¦135663, and \add ≈I'll definitely be expected to\add* sit with¦135674 the king¦135670 to eat¦135671,” David¦135656 told \add *him\add*. “\add ≈But¦135655\add* you \add should¦135672\add* let me go¦135672, and I'll hide¦135673 myself¦135673 in¦135674 the countryside¦135674 until¦135675 the third¦135677 evening¦135676.\x + \xo 20:5: \xt Num 28:11.\x*
\v 6 If your father misses¦135682 me, then tell \add <him\add*, ‘David¦135688 \add ≈begged me to let him go to his home in\add* Bethlehem, to \add ≈join his clan for their yearly\add* sacrifice¦135695.’
\v 7 If \add @your father\add* says, ‘That's fine,’ then your servant¦135708 \add ≈can relax\add*. \add ≈But¦135709 if he gets really¦135711 angry\add*, \add ≈you'll know¦135714 that he's planning\add* evil¦135718.
\v 8 \add If that's the case,\add* \add ≈it'll be up to you to do what's right with¦135727 your servant in¦135727 terms of our agreement¦135722 before\add* Yahweh¦135728. If \add ≈I've been disloyal to the king,\add* kill me yourself—\add ≈no need to drag me to him\add*.
\p
\v 9 “\add ≈Don't¦135769 even think of it\add*,” Yonatan \add ≈responded¦135751\add*. “If I find out that my father¦135766 \add ≈has decided to harm\add* you, I'll¦135760 \add ≈certainly\add* tell you.”
\p
\v 10 “If your father \add ≈does have a cruel plan\add*, who'll tell me?” David¦135776 \add ≈asked\add* Yonatan.
\p
\v 11 “Come \add ≈with me\add* out to the countryside.” Yonatan \add ≈replied\add*. \add ≈So\add* they¦135799 went¦135799 out to the countryside together
\v 12 and \add *he\add* \add ≈promised\add* David¦135808, “By Yahweh¦135809 the god¦135810 of Yisrael¦135811, I will definitely sound¦135814 out my father¦135817 around this time¦135818 tomorrow¦135820, or \add ≈soon after\add*. If \add ≈it looks\add* good¦135824 for \add *you\add*, then I'll¦135814 certainly send¦135831 \add ≈a message to let you know\add*.
\v 13 \add But¦135843\add* if \add *he's\add* planning evil for you, may Yahweh punish me similarly or even worse if I don't inform you, and send you away so you can leave in peace¦135861. And may Yahweh be with you just like he used to be with my father.
\v 14 \add ≈But¦135871\add* as long as I'm still¦135874 alive¦135875, please \add keep our\add* \add ≈agreement¦135880 of faithfulness before\add* Yahweh¦135881 so I won't¦135871 die¦135883.
\v 15 Don't¦135902 ever \add ≈terminate\add* that agreement¦135890 of faithfulness towards my family, even when Yahweh¦135898 \add ≈destroys\add* your¦135890 enemies¦135901 \add ≈wherever they are\add*.\x + \xo 20:15: \xt 2Sam 9:1.\x*
\v 16 \add ≈So¦135908\add* Yonatan made¦135908 a \add ≈multi-generational\add* agreement with David, \add adding\add*, “And may Yahweh¦135915 \add ≈punish David's¦135913 enemies¦135917\add*.”
\p
\v 17 Then¦135920 Yonatan¦135921 got David¦135925 to promise again¦135920 because¦135926 he loved him as much as he loved his \add ≈own life¦135931\add*,
\v 18 and \add *he\add* told him, “The¦135938 new moon¦135940 \add celebration\add* is tomorrow¦135939, and you'll be missed¦135941 because¦135942 your¦135944 seat¦135944 will be empty¦135943.
\v 19 The¦135962 following day¦135957, go¦135949 to the place¦135952 where¦135953 you hid¦135955 \add ≈before\add* and¦135946 stay¦135959 by the \add ≈big\add* rock¦135961.
\v 20 I'll¦135968 shoot¦135968 three¦135965 arrows¦135966 to the side¦135967 of it¦135967, as if \add ≈trying to hit\add* a target¦135972.
\v 21 Then¦135974 I'll¦135975 send¦135975 \add &my\add* boy to find the arrows. If I tell \add *him\add* that they're on my side, then¦135974 just as Yahweh¦136005 lives \add ≈it'll be safe for\add* you¦135992 to come¦135996 \add out\add*.
\v 22 \add ≈But¦136007\add* if I¦136010 tell the boy that they're on the far side, go¦136016 because¦136017 Yahweh¦136019 is sending you away.
\v 23 As¦136024 for \add ≈our pact\add*, Yahweh¦136027 \add ≈will watch over us and¦136021 our descendants\add*.”
\p
\v 24 \add ≈So\add* David¦136036 hid¦136035 himself¦136035 in the countryside¦136037, and \add at\add* the new moon¦136039 \add celebrations\add*, the king¦136041 sat¦136040 down to eat¦136046—
\v 25 sitting at¦136055 his \add ≈usual\add* seat by the wall¦136059. Yonatan \add ≈sat opposite\add*, and¦136048 Abner¦136063 sat beside Sha'ul¦136065. \add ≈No one was sitting in¦136055 David's¦136068 seat\add*
\v 26 \add ≈but¦136070\add* Sha'ul¦136073 didn't¦136070 say anything¦136074 that day¦136075, \add ≈thinking\add*, “\add ≈Something must have happened that made him\add* \add ceremonially\add* unclean.”
\v 27 \add ≈However,\add* the next day of the \add ≈celebration\add* when David¦136096 was still missing, Sha'ul¦136099 asked his son Yonatan, “Why didn't¦136105 Yishay's¦136110 son come to the meal yesterday¦136113 or today?”
\p
\v 28 “David¦136128 \add ≈begged\add* me \add ≈to let him go to\add* Bethlehem,” Yonatan answered¦136121.
\v 29 “He \add ≈requested\add*, ‘Please \add ≈release me\add* because¦136138 our clan's sacrifice¦136139 will be in the city¦136142, and my brother has \add ≈told¦136144 me to be there¦136160\add*. \add ≈So¦136135\add* now, if I've¦136151 found¦136151 favour¦136152 in your eyes¦136153, please let me slip away and \add ≈go¦136136 to\add* see¦136156 my brothers¦136159.’ \add ≈That's why\add* he hasn't come¦136165 to the king's¦136169 table¦136168.”
\p
\v 30 Sha'ul¦136175 \add ≈was furious at\add* Yonatan and \add ≈yelled at\add* him, “\add You\add* son of a perverse¦136181, rebellious¦136182 woman! Do \add you think that\add* I don't¦136183 know that you're \add ≈taking the side of\add* Yishay's¦136191 son? It's to your \add ≈detriment\add* and to the shame of your mother's¦136195 nakedness¦136194,
\v 31 because as long as that¦136201 son of Yishay¦136204 stays alive on this earth, you and your kingdom¦136212 will never be established¦136210. Now¦136216, send¦136214 \add men\add* and \add ≈arrest\add* him, because he's \add ≈on his way to the grave\add*!”
\p
\v 32 “Why should he be put to death¦136234?” Yonatan \add ≈retorted\add*. “What \add wrong\add* has he done¦136236?”
\p
\v 33 Then¦136238 Sha'ul¦136239 hurled¦136238 his spear¦136242 at him to \add ≈kill him\add*, and¦136238 Yonatan¦136246 knew \add then¦136238\add* that his father¦136252 had \add already\add* \add ≈determined¦136249 to kill\add* David¦136256.
\v 34 Yonatan angrily got up from the table¦136262. He didn't¦136266 eat any food that second¦136272 day¦136269 of the \add ≈celebration\add*—he was upset about David because¦136274 his father¦136281 had dishonoured him.\f + \fr 20:34 \ft It's not entirely clear who this ‘him’ was (as Sha'ul had seriously dishonoured Yonatan as well), so we've left it ambiguous (although David was certainly the last name mentioned).\f*
\v 35 \add ≈So it was that the following\add* morning¦136285, Yonatan went¦136286 out to the countryside¦136288 \add ≈at¦136285 the time¦136289 agreed\add* with David¦136290, taking a small¦136292 boy¦136291 \add with him\add*.
\v 36 “Run \add ahead\add*—find the arrows that¦136303 I'm about¦136305 to shoot¦136305,” he told his boy. The boy ran¦136307 and¦136295 he shot the arrow to \add ≈go over his head\add*.
\v 37 The boy went¦136314 to where the arrow was, \add ≈but¦136314\add* Yonatan called¦136323 out, “Isn't the arrow \add ≈further past\add* you¦136330?”
\v 38 Then¦136333 he \add ≈added\add*, “\add ≈Hurry up! Don't¦136339 mess around\add*.” \add ≈So¦136333\add* the boy¦136336 picked¦136342 up the arrow¦136347 and¦136333 brought it back to his master¦136352,
\v 39 \add ≈but¦136354\add* the boy¦136354 didn't¦136355 \add ≈realise what had just happened\add*—only Yonatan¦136360 and¦136354 David¦136361 knew.
\v 40 Then¦136367 Yonatan¦136368 gave¦136367 his equipment to the boy¦136374 and¦136378 \add ≈sent him back to\add* the city¦136382.
\p
\v 41 \add When\add* the boy¦136384 \add ≈was gone\add*, David came up from the side and \add ≈dropped onto his knees\add*, bowing three¦136394 times¦136395 with his face¦136391 to the ground. Then they \add ≈greeted each other with a kiss and cried together, although David cried the most\add*.
\v 42 Then Yonatan told David, “Go in peace¦136416, because us two¦136419 have \add ≈made a pact together\add* in Yahweh's¦136422 name¦136421, \add ≈agreeing\add*, ‘Yahweh \add will watch\add* between me and you¦136428, and between my descendants and your descendants forever¦136435.’ ” Then \add @David\add* set off and Yonatan \add ≈went back\add* to the city.
\c 21
\s1 David stays at Nob
\rem /s1 David Flees from Saul; David Runs from Saul; David at Nob; David and the Holy Bread
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 David \add ≈went¦136442\add* to the priest Ahimelek at Nob, \add ≈but¦136439\add* Ahimelek trembled \add ≈when he saw\add* David and \add ≈asked\add* him, “Why are you \add here\add* by yourself? \add ≈How come you don't have a companion\add*?”\x + \xo 21:1-6: \xt Mat 12:3-4; Mrk 2:25-26; Luk 6:3.\x*
\p
\v 2 “The king \add ≈sent me on an errand\add*,” David¦136447 \add ≈replied\add*, “\add ≈but¦136446\add* he told me \add ≈not to tell anyone¦136462 about the matter that he sent me on\add*. \add ≈Plus\add* I've \add ≈told my young men where to meet me\add*.
\v 3 \add ≈Now, what food do you have on\add* hand? Give me five loaves of bread or whatever you can find.”
\p
\v 4 “There is no ordinary bread \add ≈on¦136512\add* hand,” the priest answered. “But¦136501 there¦136504 is the sacred bread, if only the young men have kept themselves from women.”
\p
\v 5 “\add ≈There's definitely been no women¦136543 near us for\add* three days,” David¦136521 told the priest¦136518. “\add ≈That's how I always do missions\add*. The men\f + \fr 21:5 \ft The Hebrew says ‘the men's vessels’ (or ‘containers’). It's not culturally clear if that referred to their ‘bodies’ (as this translation implies) or to something they carried.\f* have been pure, even though it was a common journey, and today \add ≈even more so\add*.”
\p
\v 6 \add ≈So\add* the priest¦136551 gave him the sacred¦136575 bread, because¦136554 there was no other bread there other than the \add previous day's\add* bread of the presence which had been removed from \add ≈in¦136563 front\add* of Yahweh to be replaced by fresh bread.\x + \xo 21:6: \xt Lev 24:5-9.\x*
\p
\v 7 \add Now it so happened\add* that one of Sha'ul's servants was there¦136588 that day¦136602 \add ≈presenting¦136595 himself to\add* Yahweh¦136598. He was Doeg the Edomite, a \add ≈leader\add* of Sha'ul's herdsmen.
\p
\v 8 David \add ≈asked\add* Ahimelek, “\add ≈Do you have\add* any spear or sword here? \add ≈I didn't have time to grab my sword or my weapons when I left\add*, because the king's matter was urgent.”
\p
\v 9 “\add I've only got\add* Goliat's sword,” \add ≈replied\add* the priest. “\add From\add* the Philistine who you \add ≈killed\add* in the Elah valley. Look, it's wrapped in the cloak behind the sacred apron. If you want to take it for yourself, take \add <it\add*, because¦136638 there's no other \add ≈sword around\add* here.”
\p “\add Yes,\add* there's no \add ≈other sword\add* like it,” said David¦136625. “\add ≈Get it for\add* me.”\x + \xo 21:9: \xt 1Sam 17:51.\x*
\s1 David flees to Gat
\rem /s1 David Flees to Gath; David at Gath
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 Then David¦136690 left and \add continued¦136659\add* \add ≈moving to distance himself\add* from Sha'ul, and he went to King Akish of Gat.
\v 11 \add ≈But¦136697\add* Akish's servants \add ≈complained\add* to him, “Isn't that David \add who'll become\add* king¦136709 of \add ≈Israel\add*? \add ≈Isn't he the one they sing about when they dance, chanting\add*, ‘Sha'ul¦136704 has \add ≈killed\add* his thousands, and David his tens of thousands?’ ”\x + \xo 21:11: \xt 1Sam 18:7; 29:5.\x*
\p
\v 12 David¦136720 \add ≈listened to those words without any reaction because\add* he was very afraid of \add what\add* King¦136721 Akish \add might do¦136725\add*,\x + \xo 21:12: \xt Psa 56 header.\x*
\v 13 \add ≈so\add* he changed his behaviour \add ≈when he was with¦136743 them\add* and pretended to be insane. He \add ≈scratched\add* on¦136743 \add wooden\add* doors and let his saliva \add ≈dribble\add* down his beard.\x + \xo 21:13: \xt Psa 34 header.\x*
\v 14 Then \add King\add* Akish \add ≈complained\add* to his servants, “Look, \add ≈that man's\add* insane. Why did you all bring him to me?
\v 15 \add ≈Don't I already have enough madmen around here—why add another? Must that man come\add* into my house?”
\c 22
\s1 David moves around but gains followers
\rem /s1 David as outlaw captain; The Slaughter of the Priests; David at Adullam and Mizpah; David and His Followers at Adullam; David at the Cave of Adullam
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add ≈So\add* David¦136807 left there and slipped away to the cave at Adullam¦136813. \add ≈When his brothers¦136815 and other relatives found out\add*, they went and \add joined\add* him there.\x + \xo 22:1: \xt Psa 57 header; Psa 142 header.\x*
\v 2 \add Over time,\add* any man \add ≈who was in trouble\add*, or \add ≈in debt\add*, or \add ≈discontented\add* gathered¦136824 \add ≈around\add* him, and¦136824 he became \add ≈their leader\add*—some four hundred¦136849 men \add in all\add*.
\p
\v 3 From there¦136854, David¦136853 went¦136852 to Mitspah in Moab, and¦136852 he \add ≈asked\add* the king¦136861 of Moab, “Please let my \add ≈parents\add* \add ≈stay here in the palace\add* with you until¦136869 \add ≈I find out\add* what God¦136877 will do¦136874 for me.”
\v 4 \add ≈So¦136879\add* he \add ≈took them to\add* the king¦136883 \add ≈where\add* they stayed with¦136893 him \add ≈the entire¦136887 time¦136889 that David¦136892 lived¦136885\add* in¦136893 the fortress.
\p
\v 5 Then the prophet¦136898 Gad¦136897 told David, “Don't¦136902 stay in the fortress. \add ≈Leave it\add* and come back to \add ≈Yehudah's¦136910 region¦136909\add*.” \add ≈So\add* David left and went¦136911 to the Heret¦136915 forest¦136914 \add in Yehudah\add*.
\s1 The slaughter of the priests
\rem /s1 Saul Kills the Priests of Nob; Saul Slaughters the Priests at Nob
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 6 \add One¦136926 day,\add* Sha'ul¦136919 was sitting¦136927 holding his spear¦136933 underneath¦136929 the tamarisk¦136931 tree¦136931 on¦136928 a hill \add near\add* Gibeah¦136928 with¦136928 all his servants¦136937 stationed \add ≈around\add* him, \add ≈when\add* \add *he\add* heard \add ≈where\add* David¦136922 and his men¦136923 were,
\v 7 and he \add ≈accused\add* his servants¦136943, “Listen now, you Benyamites. Will Yishay's¦136957 son also\f + \fr 22:7 \ft See note on ‘םַג’ (‘gam’) at https://www.sil.org/resources/publications/entry/99313.\f* give¦136954 fields¦136958 and vineyards¦136959 to all of you? Will he make all of you commanders of thousands¦136963 and commanders of hundreds¦136965?
\v 8 \add Yes,\add* all of you have conspired¦136968 against me. \add ≈Not one of you informed me when\add* my \add own\add* son made a \add ≈pact\add* with¦136977 \add that\add* son of Yishay¦136984. And none¦136985 of you \add ≈took my side or even informed me\add* that my son has \add ≈encouraged\add* my servant¦136999 to lie in¦136977 ambush¦137001 against me as he is today!”
\p
\v 9 \add ≈Now\add* Doeg¦137007 the Edomite was stationed there with Sha'ul's servants¦137013, and he \add spoke up\add*, “I saw¦137017 Yishay's¦137022 son go to Nob¦137024 and talk with Ahimelek the priest,\x + \xo 22:9-10: \xt 1Sam 21:7-9; Psa 52 header.\x*
\v 10 and \add @the priest\add* \add ≈asked Yahweh¦137035 for directions\add* for him, and gave him food and Goliat's¦137041 sword¦137040.”
\rem /s1 The Slaughter of the Priests
\p
\v 11 Then¦137046 the king \add ≈summoned\add* Ahitub's¦137054 son Ahimelek¦137051 and all his relatives who were priests¦137055 to Nob¦137063, and they all came to the king.
\v 12 “Listen now, son of Ahitub¦137078,” said Sha'ul¦137072.
\p “\add ≈Go ahead\add* my master¦137081,” \add ≈answered\add* Ahimelek.
\p
\v 13 “Why have you all conspired¦137088 against me?” asked Sha'ul¦137086. “You gave¦137094 bread¦137097 to Yishay's¦137093 son, and a sword¦137098, and you \add ≈asked guidance from\add* God¦137101 for him¦137084, \add so now\add* he's risen up against me—lying in¦137094 ambush¦137104 this very day¦137105?”
\p
\v 14 “\add ≈Isn't David¦137119 one of your most\add* faithful¦137120 servants¦137118?” Ahimelek answered¦137109 the king. “He's the king's son-in-law and commander over your guards, and isn't he honoured¦137127 in your house¦137128?
\v 15 Did I begin¦137131 to inquire¦137132 for him from God¦137136 today? Far be it from me! Don't¦137139 let the king¦137142 \add ≈accuse\add* his servant or any of my father's¦137148 household¦137147, because¦137149 your servant doesn't know about any of this—\add ≈nothing at¦137136 all\add*.”
\p
\v 16 “For certain, Ahimelek,” the king¦137163 \add ≈declared\add*. “you and¦137162 all your father's household¦137170 will certainly die \add for that\add*!”
\v 17 Then¦137173 the king \add ≈commanded\add* his messengers who were stationed beside him, “Turn around and¦137173 put Yahweh's priests to death¦137179 because \add ≈they've taken David's¦137189 side\add*. And because they knew he was fleeing¦137194, but¦137173 they didn't¦137196 \add ≈inform me\add*.” \add ≈But¦137173\add* the king's servants¦137205 weren't willing¦137204 to attack¦137211 Yahweh's priests.
\v 18 \add ≈So\add* the king¦137217 \add ≈commanded\add* Doeg the Edomite, “You turn around and attack the priests.” \add ≈So\add* Doeg turned¦137224 and he himself attacked the priests. He \add ≈killed¦137232\add* eighty-five men that day¦137234 who \add ≈wore\add* sacred linen¦137241 aprons.
\v 19 \add ≈Also¦137243\add* \add at\add* Nob¦137244 (the city¦137245 of priests¦137247) he \add ≈killed\add* men and women \add ≈with his\add* sword, children¦137256 and infants, cattle, donkeys¦137261, and sheep¦137262.
\p
\v 20 \add ≈But¦137267\add* one of Ahimelek's sons \add ≈was able to slip away\add*. His name¦137275 was Evyatar and¦137267 he fled¦137277 \add to join\add* David¦137279,
\v 21 and¦137281 \add ≈informed\add* \add *him\add* that Sha'ul¦137286 had \add commanded Doeg to\add* kill Yahweh's¦137289 priests¦137288.
\v 22 “I knew¦137294 on that day¦137295,” David¦137292 \add ≈responded¦137291\add*, “when Doeg¦137300 the Edomite was there¦137299, that he would \add ≈probably\add* tell Sha'ul¦137307. \add ≈It's my fault that your father and¦137291 all his family were murdered\add*.
\v 23 Stick with me—don't¦137318 be afraid¦137320, because¦137321 \add ≈the one who wants to kill me would kill you¦137335 as well\add*. You'll be safe with me.”
\c 23
\s1 David rescues Ke'ilah
\rem /s1 David Saves the Town of Keilah; David Saves the City of Keilah; David Protects the Town of Keilah
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add Some time later,\add* David¦137339 was told, “Listen, the Philistines¦137342 are fighting¦137343 against¦137344 \add the city of\add* Ke'ilah and¦137345 they¦137345 are \add ≈stealing grain from\add* the threshing¦137349 floors¦137349,”
\v 2 \add ≈so\add* he asked Yahweh, “Should I go and attack those Philistines¦137357?”
\p “Go and attack the Philistines and save Ke'ilah,” Yahweh \add ≈responded¦137360\add*.
\p
\v 3 \add ≈But¦137373\add* David's¦137375 men¦137374 \add ≈warned\add* him, “Listen, we're afraid here in¦137380 Yehudah¦137380, and¦137373 \add ≈we'd be even more afraid\add* if we went to Ke'ilah against¦137387 the Philistines' \add battle¦137389\add* lines¦137389.”
\v 4 \add ≈So\add* David¦137395 \add ≈double-checked\add* with¦137397 Yahweh¦137400 \add *who\add* answered¦137399, “\add ≈Get ready\add* \add and\add* go¦137403 to Ke'ilah, because¦137405 I'm about¦137408 to give¦137408 \add ≈you victory over\add* the Philistines¦137411.”
\v 5 Then¦137414 David¦137415 and his men¦137416 went¦137414 to Ke'ilah and battled against¦137420 the Philistines¦137420—\add ≈thoroughly defeating¦137427\add* them and taking their¦137424 livestock¦137424, \add ≈thus\add* saving the inhabitants¦137432 of Ke'ilah.
\p
\v 6 \add ≈Now\add* when Ahimelek's son Evyatar had fled¦137437 to David¦137444 at¦137437 Ke'ilah, he'd \add ≈brought\add* a sacred apron \add ≈with¦137437 him\add*.
\rem /s1 Saul Pursues David
\p
\v 7 Sha'ul¦137451 was told that David¦137455 had gone to Ke'ilah, \add ≈so\add* he said¦137457, “\add Ah,\add* God¦137461 has \add ≈deserted him and¦137450 given him to me\add* because¦137452 he's shut himself¦137464 in¦137462 by \add ≈going¦137465\add* into a \add walled\add* city¦137466 with¦137462 doors¦137467 \add ≈that can be barred shut\add*.”
\v 8 \add ≈So¦137470\add* Sha'ul¦137471 \add ≈initiated preparations\add* to go¦137478 to Ke'ilah¦137479 to entrap David¦137483 and¦137484 his men¦137486.
\p
\v 9 \add ≈But¦137488\add* David¦137489 \add ≈discovered\add* that Sha'ul¦137492 was \add ≈planning to destroy\add* him, \add ≈so\add* he \add ≈summoned\add* Evyatar the priest¦137499, “Bring the sacred apron here.”
\v 10 Then David¦137505 \add ≈prayed\add*, “Yahweh¦137506, God¦137507 of Yisrael¦137508, your¦137511 servant¦137511 has heard¦137510 that Sha'ul¦137515 is \add ≈intending\add* to come¦137516 to Ke'ilah—to destroy¦137520 the city¦137521 because¦137522 of me.
\v 11 Will Sha'ul¦137529 come \add ≈here\add*, just as your servant has heard¦137531? Will the masters of Ke'ilah \add ≈turn me over to him\add*? Yahweh, God¦137534 of Yisrael¦137535, please¦137538 tell your servant.”
\p “\add Yes,\add* he will \add ≈go there\add*,” Yahweh \add ≈answered\add*.
\p
\v 12 “\add And\add* will the masters of Ke'ilah \add ≈hand¦137554\add* me and¦137545 my men¦137553 \add ≈over to\add* Sha'ul¦137556?” David¦137546 \add ≈asked\add* \add again\add*.
\p And Yahweh¦137558 said, “They will deliver¦137559 up.”
\rem /s1 David Hides in the Wilderness
\p
\v 13 \add ≈So\add* David and his men¦137564 (about 600 \add ≈of them\add*), \add ≈left\add* Ke'ilah and \add ≈moved around from place to place\add*. \add ≈When\add* Sha'ul¦137574 was told that David had slipped away from Ke'ilah, he¦137578 \add ≈didn't continue with¦137572 his plans\add*.
\s1 Sha'ul pursues David
\rem /s1 David in the Hill Country
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 14 David¦137585 stayed in \add various\add* strongholds¦137587 in the wilderness, and¦137584 \add sometimes\add* he stayed in the hill country¦137589 in the Zif¦137592 wilderness. Sha'ul¦137594 \add ≈kept searching for them\add*, but¦137584 God¦137601 didn't¦137598 \add ≈allow him to capture them\add*.
\rem /s1 David Eludes Saul in the Wilderness
\v 15 \add Once\add* David¦137605 was \add staying\add* in¦137616 the Zif¦137618 wilderness¦137616 and¦137615 he¦137609 saw¦137604 that Sha'ul¦137610 had \add ≈arrived there to try to kill him\add*.
\v 16 Then Sha'ul's son Yonatan went¦137627 to David¦137630 at¦137636 Horesh¦137631 and \add ≈encouraged¦137632 him to keep trusting\add* God¦137636,
\v 17 saying, “Don't¦137640 be afraid¦137642, because¦137643 my father Sha'ul¦137647 won't¦137640 \add ≈capture\add* you. As for you, you'll reign¦137650 over Yisrael¦137653, and¦137638 I'll¦137655 be \add ≈your second¦137658 in command\add*. Even my father knows \add ≈that\add*.”
\v 18 \add Again,\add* the two of them \add ≈made¦137666 an agreement¦137668\add* before Yahweh¦137670. Then¦137666 David¦137672 stayed at¦137673 Horesh¦137673 and¦137666 Yonatan returned home¦137676.\x + \xo 23:18: \xt 1Sam 18:3.\x*
\p
\v 19 \add Some men\add* \add ≈from Zif\add* went¦137679 to Sha'ul¦137683 at¦137690 Gibeah¦137684, saying, “\add ≈We're pretty sure that¦137694\add* David's¦137687 hiding¦137688 himself¦137688 among¦137689 us in the strongholds¦137690 in Horesh¦137691, on¦137690 the Hakilah¦137693 hill south¦137695 of Yeshimon¦137696.\x + \xo 23:19: \xt Psa 54 header.\x*
\v 20 \add ≈So¦137698\add* now, \add ≈your majesty\add*, come \add ≈over whenever you want\add*, and¦137698 \add ≈we'll make sure he's handed over to you\add*.”
\p
\v 21 “May you all be blessed¦137713 by Yahweh¦137715,” Sha'ul¦137712 responded¦137711, “because¦137716 you've had compassion¦137717 on me.
\v 22 Please go back and \add ≈double-check\add* and \add find out\add* who's seen¦137734 him¦137734 there¦137735. \add ≈Learn more\add* and \add ≈look at the place¦137729 yourselves\add*, because¦137736 I've been told that he's very crafty.
\v 23 \add ≈Find out all the other\add* places¦137746 where¦137747 he hides¦137748 himself¦137748. Return to me with definite \add information\add*, then I'll go¦137755 with you all, and¦137743 if he's there¦137749 in¦137764 that \add ≈region\add*, then I'll search¦137762 for him among¦137764 the \add ≈population\add* of Yehudah¦137766.”
\p
\v 24 \add ≈So¦137768\add* they \add ≈left and returned\add* to Zif¦137770 ahead of Sha'ul¦137772. \add At¦137777 that time,\add* David¦137773 and his men¦137774 were in¦137775 the Maon¦137776 wilderness¦137775 south¦137779 of Yeshimon¦137780
\v 25 \add ≈when\add* Sha'ul and his men¦137784 came to search¦137785 \add for them\add*. \add ≈When¦137782\add* David¦137787 was told, he went \add further south\add* to a \add ≈rocky hill therein that\add* wilderness, \add ≈but¦137782\add* Sha'ul \add also\add* heard \add that\add* and followed \add ≈them\add*.
\v 26 \add At one point,\add* Sha'ul was on one side of a hill¦137805, and David and his men were on the other¦137811 side. David was hurrying¦137814 to get away from Sha'ul, but¦137802 Sha'ul and his men were trying to surround¦137820 David and his men to capture¦137827 them.
\v 27 \add Just\add* then¦137829, a messenger¦137829 \add ≈arrived for\add* Sha'ul¦137833, saying, “\add ≈Come quickly\add* because¦137837 the Philistines¦137840 have raided \add ≈our region\add*.”
\v 28 \add ≈So¦137845\add* Sha'ul¦137846 \add ≈had to give up\add* chasing¦137847 David¦137849 and¦137845 went¦137850 to \add ≈repel\add* the Philistines¦137852.
\v 29 David \add also\add* \add ≈left that area\add* and stayed in the strongholds \add ≈around\add* Eyn-Gedi.
\c 24
\s1 David refuses to kill Sha'ul
\rem /s1 David Spares Saul's Life
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add ≈After\add* Sha'ul returned from \add ≈repelling\add* the Philistines, they told him that David¦137864 was \add now\add* in¦137867 the Eyn-Gedi wilderness.
\v 2 \add ≈So\add* Sha'ul¦137876 \add ≈chose\add* three thousand \add ≈Israeli warriors\add* and went \add ≈searching\add* for David¦137883 and his men at¦137884 Wild Goats Rocks.
\v 3 On¦137898 the way, as \add #they\add* reached the sheep yards, Sha'ul¦137891 \add ≈went¦137899 into a cave to relieve himself, but¦137890\add* David¦137903 and his men¦137904 were \add ≈hiding there further inside\add* the cave.\x + \xo 24:3: \xt Psa 57 header; Psa 142 header.\x*
\v 4 David's¦137928 men¦137929 \add ≈whispered\add* to him, “Wow, today what Yahweh told you \add ≈is coming true\add* \add when he said\add*, ‘Listen, I'm about to give \add ≈victory over your enemy\add*, and you will do to him \add ≈whatever you think is right\add*.’ ” Then David \add ≈sneaked up\add* and \add ≈quietly\add* cut \add a piece\add* off the edge of Sha'ul's robe.
\v 5 \add ≈Afterwards\add* \add however,\add* David¦137937 \add ≈felt bad because\add* he'd cut¦137961 \add a piece\add* off Sha'ul's \add ≈clothes\add*
\v 6 and he said to his men, “\add ≈By Yahweh, I shouldn't have done that¦137988\add* to my master—to Yahweh's anointed \add king\add*—to \add ≈do him harm\add* because he's the \add one\add* Yahweh anointed.”\x + \xo 24:6: \xt 1Sam 26:11.\x*
\v 7 \add *He\add* restrained his men¦137994 with \add a few quiet\add* words and \add ≈wouldn't allow them to attack\add* Sha'ul.
\p Then Sha'ul left the cave and \add ≈headed down the path\add*.
\v 8 After \add ≈waiting a few moment\add*, David went¦138033 out of the cave and called out behind Sha'ul, “My master the king.” \add ≈When\add* Sha'ul turned and looked behind him, David bowed
\v 9 and said, “Why do you listen to \add ≈people when they say¦138052 that I'm wanting to harm you\add*?
\v 10 Look, \add ≈you can see for yourself\add* that Yahweh gave \add ≈me the opportunity to harm\add* you today in the cave, and some \add of my men\add* wanted me to kill you, but¦138066 I \add ≈showed you\add* compassion and \add ≈told them that\add* I wouldn't \add ≈do¦138070 harm to\add* my master, because he's Yahweh's \add ≈chosen king\add*.
\v 11 \add ≈Your majesty, look of this piece of your robe that I'm holding\add*, because¦138107 when I cut it off, I didn't¦138102 kill you. \add ≈So now you can see for yourself that I have no evil plans, and I haven't do you any wrong, even though\add* you're \add ≈ambushing me so you can kill me\add*.
\v 12 May Yahweh judge between you and me \add ≈because¦138123 I'll let Yahweh repay you for any harm against me—it won't¦138129 be me who works\add* against you.
\v 13 As the ancient proverb says, ‘Evil things are done by evil people.’ \add ≈But¦138153 it¦138159 won't¦138158 be me who harms\add* you.
\v 14 Who has Yisrael's king come \add ≈to find\add*? Who are you chasing after? A dead dog? A flea?\x + \xo 24:14: \xt 1Sam 26:20.\x*
\v 15 May Yahweh \add ≈take his judgement seat\add* and judge between me and you, and may he see my case and \add ≈find me innocent and save me from you\add*.”
\p
\v 16 \add ≈When David finished saying that to Sha'ul, Sha'ul called back\add*, “Is that your voice, my son David?” Then \add *he\add* \add ≈cried loudly\add*
\v 17 and told David¦138214, “You're more righteous than I \add am\add*, because you \add ≈were\add* good to me \add ≈even when I\add* did evil \add things\add* to you.
\v 18 You've \add ≈stated\add* that you've¦138244 \add ≈treated me well\add*—how Yahweh \add ≈gave you the advantage over me, yet\add* you didn't kill me.
\v 19 \add ≈Now\add* if a man finds his enemy \add unprepared\add*, does he \add just\add* \add ≈send him off happily\add*? \add ≈Well,\add* may Yahweh¦138264 reward you for \add ≈your mercy towards\add* me today.
\v 20 Now listen, I know for certain that you're going to \add ≈become king\add* and that the kingdom of Yisrael will \add ≈prosper under your leadership\add*.
\v 21 Now \add ≈promise\add* me by Yahweh that you¦138296 won't \add ≈destroy my name from my family line by killing my descendants\add*.”
\v 22 David promised \add <that\add* to Sha'ul, then Sha'ul \add ≈went back home\add* and David and his men returned to \add &their\add* stronghold.
\c 25
\s1 David, Nabal, and Abigil
\rem /s1 Death of Samuel; The Death of Samuel; David, Nabal and Abigail
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add Later on¦138350,\add* Shemuel died¦138340 and all \add ≈the Israelis¦138343\add* gathered¦138342 and mourned¦138346 for him, then they buried¦138349 him at¦138350 his house¦138350 in¦138350 Ramah¦138351.
\rem /s1 Nabal Angers David; David and Abigail; David and the Wife of Nabal
\p Then David¦138353 left and went to the Paran¦138358 wilderness¦138357.
\v 2 \add ≈Now\add* there was a man in Maon¦138362 whose¦138363 business¦138363 was in Carmel. \add ≈He owned\add* three¦138370 thousand sheep and one thousand goats¦138374, and was \add busy\add* shearing¦138376 his sheep at¦138362 Carmel.
\v 3 The man's name was Nabal¦138384, and his wife was Abigail¦138387. The wife was \add ≈wise and very good-looking, whereas\add* the man (who was a Calebite) was harsh and cruel.
\p
\v 4 \add ≈While¦138409\add* David¦138403 was in¦138404 the wilderness¦138404, he heard that Nabal¦138408 was shearing¦138407 his sheep¦138411,
\v 5 \add ≈so he told\add* ten young¦138416 men, “Go to Nabal¦138425 at¦138429 Carmel¦138421 and¦138413 \add ≈greet¦138426\add* him in¦138429 my name.
\v 6 Then¦138432 tell him, ‘\add ≈Make you live long\add*. Peace to you and your¦138437 \add ≈household¦138437\add* and \add ≈everything that belongs to\add* you.
\v 7 Now I've¦138446 heard¦138446 those shearers¦138448 \add ≈are working for\add* you, and¦138459 your shepherds¦138451 have been \add ≈living\add* with¦138468 us. We haven't harmed them or \add ≈taken anything¦138463 of theirs¦138467\add* while they've been in¦138468 Carmel¦138468.
\v 8 Ask your \add ≈servants\add* and they'll \add ≈confirm this\add*. May \add these\add* young¦138477 men¦138477 \add ≈enjoy your\add* favour¦138478 because¦138480 \add harvesting the wool\add* is a \add ≈prosperous time\add*, so I request a gift for your servants and for your ‘son’, David¦138496.’ ”
\p
\v 9 \add ≈So¦138498\add* David's¦138500 men¦138499 went¦138498 and \add ≈passed all that onto\add* Nabal¦138504, and then¦138498 \add ≈waited¦138511 for his response\add*.
\v 10 \add ≈But¦138513\add* Nabal¦138514 answered¦138513 \add *them\add*, “\add ≈Why should I care about\add* David¦138518? Yishay's¦138525 son \add ≈isn't anyone¦138530 important\add*. There's lot of servants \add ≈these days\add* who are \add ≈on the run from their¦138532\add* \add #masters\add*.
\v 11 This bread¦138537 and water¦138540, and the meat that¦138543 I've¦138544 \add ≈butchered\add*, is for my shearers¦138545. Why should I give¦138546 it to men¦138547 when I don't¦138549 even know¦138550 where¦138551 they're from?”
\p
\v 12 David's¦138558 men¦138556 \add ≈left\add* and¦138555 returned¦138560 \add ≈to inform him what\add* \add Nabal\add* \add ≈had been said\add*.
\v 13 Then¦138568 David told his men, “Strap on \add ≈your swords\add*.” \add ≈So¦138568 David took\add* around four hundred men, \add ≈leaving\add* around two hundred with \add &their\add* gear.
\p
\v 14 \add Meanwhile\add*, Nabal's¦138604 wife¦138603 Abigail had been informed by a \add ≈servant\add*, “Listen, David¦138613 sent¦138612 messengers¦138614 from the wilderness¦138616 to greet¦138617 our master¦138620, \add ≈but¦138602\add* he yelled at¦138622 them.
\v 15 \add &Their\add* men¦138624 had been very good¦138625 to us—we were never harmed or \add ≈had anything¦138633 go missing\add* when we were in¦138639 the countryside¦138640.
\v 16 They \add ≈protected\add* us both day¦138650 and night¦138647 \add ≈for the whole time¦138653 that we¦138654 were\add* tending the sheep¦138657.
\v 17 \add ≈So¦138659\add* now \add ≈you should think about it and decide what you can do¦138664\add*, because¦138665 \add no doubt\add* \add ≈disaster is about to hit\add* our master¦138671 and all his household¦138675. He's a \add ≈terrible man who listens to no one\add*.”
\p
\v 18 \add ≈So¦138683\add* Abigail¦138684 quickly \add collected\add* two hundred loaves of bread¦138688, two skins of wine, five butchered sheep¦138694, a large basket of roasted¦138700 grain¦138700, a hundred bunches¦138702 of raisins¦138702, and¦138683 two hundred cakes¦138704 of figs. She loaded¦138705 \add it all\add* on donkeys¦138708
\v 19 and¦138710 told her \add ≈servants¦138711\add*, “\add ≈Go ahead\add* \add with the donkeys\add*. I'll come \add along\add* behind you all.” \add ≈But¦138710 she¦138720 didn't¦138719 say a word to\add* her husband¦138717 Nabal¦138718.
\p
\v 20 \add ≈As¦138738 she rode down a hill¦138731 and into the ravine¦138730, suddenly\add* she encountered David¦138733 and his men¦138734 coming down to meet her.
\v 21 David¦138740 had told \add <them\add*, “\add Well¦138744\add* \add ≈it was certainly a waste of time looking after that fellow in¦138751 the wilderness¦138751\add*, and¦138740 \add ≈taking such care not to take anything¦138760 of his\add*. Then¦138740 \add ≈all he did was to return¦138761 insults for our good¦138766 behaviour\add*.
\v 22 May God¦138771 \add preserve\add* \add *my\add* enemies¦138772, if I \add ≈don't eliminate\add* all of the \add ≈older and¦138774 young men in¦138788 his household before\add* the morning¦138786.
\rem /s1 Abigail Intercedes for Nabal
\p
\v 23 \add ≈When¦138790\add* Abigail¦138791 saw¦138790 David, she \add ≈quickly dismounted\add* from the donkey¦138798 and fell¦138799 \add ≈to her knees in front¦138800\add* of David and bowed¦138805 \add her face¦138804\add* to the ground¦138806.
\v 24 She \add ≈clutched\add* his feet¦138811 and¦138808 said, “My master¦138816, \add ≈let the guilt¦138817 be\add* on¦138813 me myself. Please let your female servant \add ≈explain to you\add*—\add ≡please\add* \add ≈listen¦138823 to what\add* your female servant \add ≈has to say\add*.
\v 25 My master, please¦138830 \add ≈don't¦138856 remain angry at that worthless¦138840\add* man, Nabal¦138844. His name \add ≈means ‘fool’\add* and so he is. \add ≈He's a senseless man, but¦138852\add* my master, your female servant¦138855 didn't¦138856 see the young¦138860 men you sent.
\v 26 And now, my master, as Yahweh lives and as your spirit lives, it's Yahweh who's \add ≈prevented you from spilling blood¦138877 and taking his punishment into your own hands\add*. And now may your enemies¦138884 and \add ≈anyone who makes plans\add* against¦138886 my master be like Nabal¦138883.
\v 27 Now let this gift¦138892 that¦138894 your servant¦138897 has brought¦138896 to my master be given¦138899 to the young¦138900 men¦138900 \add ≈who serve\add* my master.
\v 28 Please forgive your female¦138908 servant \add ≈for being forward\add* because¦138909 Yahweh will certain \add ≈advance my master's reputation\add*. My master is fighting¦138922 Yahweh's¦138913 battles¦138919 and \add ≈all your life, you haven't broken the law\add*.
\v 29 \add ≈Even¦138945 if someone decides to chase you and¦138930 try to kill you my master¦138939, your god¦138946 Yahweh¦138939 will keep you wrapped in¦138942 his\add* bundle¦138942 of the living¦138943, \add ≈whereas\add* your enemies¦138949 will be \add ≈slung away like stones from a sling\add*.
\v 30 If¦138959 Yahweh¦138960 does \add ≈all the good¦138967 things he's promised\add* for my master¦138960, then¦138955 \add in time\add* you'll become Yisrael's¦138973 ruler¦138970.
\v 31 \add ≈It¦138992 would be good not to have someone's unnecessary death\add* as an obstacle for you or \add ≈to be seen as\add* my master \add just\add* saving \add ≈his own skin\add*. Then¦138975 Yahweh¦138983 will \add ≈bless\add* my master, and¦138975 you will \add ≈appreciate\add* your female servant¦138997.”
\p
\v 32 “May Yisrael's¦139006 god¦139005 Yahweh¦139004 be blessed¦139003,” David¦139001 \add ≈responded¦139000\add*. “\add The one\add* who sent¦139008 you¦139008 to meet me today.
\v 33 And blessed be your¦139014 discernment¦139014 and blessed be you \add yourself\add*, \add ≈because you've¦139018 prevented me from shedding blood¦139022 today\add* and from \add ≈taking my own revenge\add*.
\v 34 \add Yes\add* indeed, as Yahweh¦139030 lives, Yisrael's¦139032 god¦139031 \add ≈prevented me from harming¦139035\add* you. If you hadn't hurried here to meet me, certainly \add ≈neither Nabal¦139048 nor any of his older or young men would have survived\add* \add to see\add* the morning¦139052 light¦139051.
\v 35 Then¦139056 David¦139057 \add ≈accepted the gifts that¦139060\add* she had brought¦139062 for him, and said, “Go \add ≈back\add* to your house¦139068 in¦139071 peace¦139067. See, I've¦139070 listened¦139070 to \add ≈what you said and granted¦139072 your request¦139073\add*.”
\p
\v 36 \add ≈When¦139075\add* Abigail¦139076 \add ≈got home\add* to Nabal¦139080, wow, \add ≈he had put on¦139085 a celebration\add* at his house¦139085 \add ≈with¦139085 a feast fit for\add* a king¦139087. \add ≈He¦139089 was feeling very contented and¦139075 was quite\add* drunk¦139093, \add ≈so\add* she didn't¦139097 tell him \add ≈anything¦139101 at all\add* until¦139104 it was light¦139106 in¦139085 the morning¦139107.
\v 37 And so it was in¦139110 the morning¦139110, when Nabal's¦139113 wine \add ≈had worn off\add*, his wife told him \add ≈what she'd done\add*. \add In his rage,\add* \add ≈he had a stroke and¦139109 became¦139126 totally paralysed\add*
\v 38 for about¦139130 then¦139129 days¦139131. Then¦139129 Yahweh¦139133 struck¦139132 Nabal¦139136 and¦139129 he died¦139137.
