\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add ≈But¦139839\add* David¦139840 \add ≈thought to himself\add*, “\add ≈One of these days, I'm going to be caught off-guard by Sha'ul's actions\add*. There's no \add ≈future\add* for me \add here\add*, \add ≈so\add* I definitely \add need to\add* slip away to the Philistines¦139863 region¦139862. Then \add hopefully\add* Sha'ul will \add ≈give up looking for\add* me again¦139868 \add ≈across\add* all Yisrael¦139872, and I'll¦139845 slip \add ≈be able to stay under his radar\add*.”
\v 2 \add ≈So¦139876\add* \add *he\add* \add ≈left\add* \add Israel\add* and crossed over with his six¦139880 hundred¦139882 men to Gat¦139893 \add and\add* King¦139892 Akish (Maok's son).
\v 3 Then¦139895 David and his men¦139902 \add ≈settled\add* with Akish in¦139900 Gat¦139900. David had his two¦139906 wives¦139907 Ahinoam¦139908 (from Yezreel¦139909) and Abigail¦139910 (\add ≈widow\add* of Nabal¦139913 from Carmel¦139914) with him.
\v 4 \add ≈When¦139916\add* Sha'ul¦139917 was told that David¦139921 had fled¦139920 to Gat¦139922, he \add ≈stopped\add* searching¦139928 for him.
\p
\v 5 \add One day,\add* David¦139932 \add ≈asked\add* Akish, “If I've¦139939 found¦139939 favour¦139940 in your eyes¦139941, \add ≈ask\add* them to give¦139942 me a place¦139945 in one of the cities¦139947 in the countryside¦139948 \add ≈so\add* I can live¦139949 there¦139950. \add ≈There's no need for us to bludge on¦139941 you¦139956 here in the royal¦139955 city¦139954\add*.”
\v 6 \add ≈So¦139958\add* Akish gave Tsiklag¦139966 to \add @David\add* that day, \add and¦139958\add* as a result, it still belongs¦139968 to Yehudah's¦139971 kings¦139970 until¦139972 today.
\p
\v 7 David¦139983 \add ended up\add* living in¦139984 the Philistine¦139985 countryside¦139984 for \add around\add* \add ≈sixteen\add* months¦139988,
\v 8 and \add during that time\add*, \add *he\add* would take his men¦139992 to raid the Geshurites¦139996, the Girzites, and the Amalekites¦139999. They had lived in \add ≈that region¦140010 from long¦140005 before\add*—from Shur¦140007 \add ≈all the way to\add* Egypt¦140011.
\v 9 When¦140013 David¦140014 \add ≈attacked¦140013 a place\add*, he would take¦140022 the sheep¦140023 and cattle¦140024, donkeys¦140025 and camels¦140026, and clothes¦140027, \add ≈but¦140013\add* he didn't¦140018 leave \add ≈anyone\add* alive. Then¦140013 he'd return¦140028 home and \add ≈visit\add* Akish¦140032.
\v 10 \add @The king\add* would ask him, “\add ≈Which place\add* did¦140038 you¦140038 raid today?”
\p David¦140041 would tell \add <him\add*, “In southern Yehudah¦140045,” or “South of the Yerahmeelites¦140049,” or “In the southern Kenite \add region\add*”.
\v 11 David¦140060 never allowed any man or woman¦140056 to live to be able to go back to Gat¦140062 and¦140055 report on¦140080 what he'd \add really\add* been doing. \add ≈That was how he handled things for the entire¦140075 time¦140077\add* that¦140078 he lived in¦140080 the Philistine countryside¦140080.
\v 12 \add ≈But¦140083\add* \add King\add* Akish was trusting¦140083 David¦140085, \add ≈thinking\add*, “He'll definitely \add ≈be hated\add* \add now by\add* his people¦140089 in¦140085 Yisrael¦140090, \add ≈so\add* he'll \add ≈be forced to remain here as my\add* servant¦140093.”
\c 28
\s1 Sha'ul consults a medium
\rem /s1 Saul and the Witch of Endor; Saul Consults a Medium
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add ≈Some time¦140098 later,\add* the Philistines¦140101 gathered¦140100 their¦140104 \add ≈forces\add* \add getting ready\add* to battle¦140105 against¦140107 Yisrael¦140107, and \add King\add* Akish \add ≈complimented\add* David¦140112, “\add ≈We'll definitely want you and your men¦140120 to join with¦140107 our forces\add*.”
\p
\v 2 “\add Sounds good\add*,” David¦140123 \add ≈replied\add*, “\add ≈because you'll be able to see for\add* yourself what your¦140134 servant¦140134 can do¦140133.”
\p “I'll¦140143 appoint \add ≈as my permanent bodyguard\add*,” Akish told \add *him\add*.
\rem /s1 Saul Consults a Medium
\p
\v 3 \add ≈By now, Shemu'el was long dead\add* and all Yisrael¦140156 had mourned¦140151 for him and had buried¦140157 him in¦140158 his town¦140159 of Ramah¦140158, and Sha'ul¦140160 had had expelled all the mediums¦140162 from the country, \add ≈along with¦140158 those who contacted the spirits¦140165\add*.\x + \xo 28:3: \xt 1Sam 25:1.\x*
\p
\v 4 Then¦140168 the Philistines¦140169 went¦140170 to Shunem¦140172 and camped \add there\add*. \add ≈Meanwhile\add* Sha'ul¦140174 gathered¦140173 all Yisrael's¦140179 \add warriors\add* and they¦140170 camped at¦140172 Gilboa¦140181,
\v 5 \add ≈but¦140183 when\add* he saw¦140183 the Philistine camp¦140187, he was afraid¦140189 and¦140183 trembled¦140190 \add ≈inside\add*.
\v 6 He asked Yahweh \add for direction\add*, but¦140194 Yahweh didn't¦140197 answer¦140198 him¦140198 either through \add #a dream¦140201\add*, nor by \add use of\add* the sacred pouch, nor through \add #any prophet¦140205\add*.\x + \xo 28:6: \xt Num 27:21.\x*
\v 7 Then¦140207 Sha'ul¦140208 \add ≈instructed\add* his servants, “Find a woman for me who \add ≈talks to the spirits of dead people\add*, \add ≈so\add* I can go to her and¦140207 \add ≈ask\add* her \add <something\add*.”
\p “Sure,” his servants told him, “there's¦140225 a woman in¦140221 Endor¦140231 \add ≈who can do that\add*.”
\p
\v 8 \add ≈So¦140233\add* Sha'ul¦140234 put on¦140255 different clothes¦140235 to disguise¦140233 himself¦140233, and went off in¦140255 the night¦140247 with two of his men¦140241. They went to the woman¦140246 and he \add ≈asked\add*, “Please divine¦140249 for me with a spirit¦140255 of the dead and bring¦140256 up the person that I tell you.”
\p
\v 9 “Listen,” said¦140264 the woman¦140265, “surely you're aware of what Sha'ul¦140274 has done¦140273—he's expelled anyone out of the country brings¦140290 back the dead or converses with¦140289 the spirits¦140282. \add ≈So¦140264\add* why are you trying to trap me? Are you trying to get me killed?”
\p
\v 10 Sha'ul¦140294 promised her \add ≈in¦140295 front of\add* Yahweh, “As Yahweh lives, you¦140302 \add ≈won't be punished¦140303 for doing this\add*.”
\p
\v 11 “\add Okay\add* then¦140307, who \add do you want me to\add* bring up for you?”
\p “Bring Shemuel up for me.” he \add ≈replied\add*.
\p
\v 12 \add ≈When¦140323\add* the woman saw Shemuel¦140327, she yelled¦140328 out and¦140323 said to Sha'ul, “\add Oh no,\add* you've tricked me! You're Sha'ul!”
\p
\v 13 “Don't¦140345 be afraid,” said the king¦140344, “but¦140342 what have you seen?”
\p “I saw gods¦140356 coming¦140358 up out of the ground,” she replied.
\p
\v 14 “What does he look like?” he \add ≈asked\add*.
\p “An old man's appearing¦140367,” she said, “wearing a robe¦140374.”
\p Sha'ul¦140376 knew¦140375 it was Shemuel¦140379, and he \add ≈knelt down\add* and bowed¦140381 \add ≈his face¦140382\add* to the ground.
\p
\v 15 “Why've you disturbed¦140393 me by bringing¦140394 me up?” Shemuel¦140388 \add ≈asked\add*.
\p “I'm very distressed¦140398,” Sha'ul¦140391 \add ≈replied\add*, “and the Philistines¦140402 are about to go to war¦140404 against¦140405 me. And God¦140406 has turned¦140407 away¦140407 from me and doesn't answer¦140411 me anymore—\add ≈he hasn't sent me any\add* prophets¦140416 or \add ≈given me any\add* dreams¦140419, \add ≈So\add* I've¦140420 called¦140420 to you \add ≈so you can tell me\add* what I should do¦140424.”
\p
\v 16 “If Yahweh's¦140431 turned¦140432 away from you¦140433 and become your¦140435 enemy¦140435,” Shemuel¦140428 \add ≈responded¦140427\add*, “\add ≈what's the point in\add* asking me?”
\v 17 \add *He's\add* \add simply\add* \add ≈done what he told me to tell you that he'd do\add*. He's ripped the kingdom¦140447 \add ≈away from you\add* and¦140437 given¦140449 it to your neighbour¦140450 David¦140451.\x + \xo 28:17: \xt 1Sam 15:28.\x*
\v 18 You didn't \add ≈do what Yahweh instructed\add* and you didn't execute¦140461 his \add ≈anger¦140464\add* against Amalek¦140465, so that's why Yahweh is doing that to you today.\x + \xo 28:18: \xt 1Sam 15:3-9.\x*
\v 19 \add *He\add* will also \add ≈allow Yisrael to be defeated by\add* the Philistines, and tomorrow¦140488 you and your sons¦140490 will be \add here\add* with¦140485 me. Yahweh¦140479 will also \add ≈allow\add* Yisrael's¦140483 \add entire\add* camp¦140495 \add of warriors\add* \add ≈to be captured by\add* the Philistines.”
\p
\v 20 Sha'ul¦140504, \add ≈who'd been standing\add* immediately¦140503 \add ≈toppled right over and¦140503 crashed\add* to the ground—he was very scared by Shemuel's¦140513 words¦140512, plus¦140505 he \add ≈didn't¦140517 have much strength¦140516\add* because¦140512 he hadn't eaten¦140523 for \add ≈twenty-four hours\add*.
\v 21 The woman¦140533 \add ≈knelt over\add* Sha'ul¦140536 and saw¦140537 that¦140555 he was terrified¦140540, and told him, “\add ≈Listen to me\add*. Your¦140547 female servant¦140546 has \add ≈risked my life¦140549 to do what you said¦140542 and follow your¦140547 instructions\add*.
\v 22 \add ≈So¦140559\add* now, \add ≈although you're the king\add*, please¦140562 listen¦140560 to your female servant¦140567. Let me get you some food¦140572, and¦140559 then¦140559 eat¦140573 it¦140574 \add ≈to regain your strength¦140576 for the journey\add* \add home\add*.
\p
\v 23 \add ≈But¦140581\add* he refused¦140581, saying, “\add No,\add* I won't¦140583 eat¦140584 \add <anything\add*.” \add ≈However\add* his servants¦140588 and also the woman¦140591 urged¦140585 him, and he \add ≈decided to take their¦140593 advice\add* \add ≈so\add* he got up from the ground¦140595 and sat¦140596 on the bed¦140599.
